# Grade 9: teaching scripts, student steps and saint stories.
R = {
'9-1-1': dict(
  saint="Augustine: the restless searcher whose Confessions begin with a prayer.",
  tryit=["List what you have wanted most.", "Mark which wants were satisfied and for how long.", "Write your own “restless heart” sentence."],
  saintStory="Augustine was born in 354 in Thagaste, North Africa. His mother, Monica, was a Christian; his father, Patricius, was not. As a student in Carthage, Augustine chased fame, pleasure and wisdom. He joined a sect, took a mistress and had a son, Adeodatus. He became a professor of rhetoric, first in Carthage, then in Rome and Milan.\nAll the while, he felt an ache that nothing filled. In Milan, he heard Ambrose preach and began to see the faith as intellectually respectable. In 386, in a garden, he heard a child’s voice: “Take up and read.” He opened Paul’s letter to the Romans and read a line about putting on Christ. He was baptized the next Easter. He wrote Confessions about ten years later, a prayer and autobiography addressed to God.\nIt opens: “You have made us for yourself, O Lord, and our heart is restless until it rests in you.” He also wrote, “Late have I loved you, O Beauty so ancient and so new.” He died in 430, as Hippo was under siege.",
  script=dict(
    open="Write on the board: “Everyone wants to be happy.” Ask the class, “Is that true? What do people try to be happy? Does it work?” List answers: money, fame, friends, grades, games, relationships. Ask, “If all those worked, would anyone still be restless?”",
    teach=["Say: “Human beings are the only creatures who ask ‘why?’ about everything. We look for truth, goodness, beauty and love, and we are never completely satisfied. The Catechism begins here: ‘The desire for God is written in the human heart, because man is created by God and for God.’ (CCC 27) This is not a feeling for religious people only. Almost every culture has prayer, sacrifice and some form of worship. Humans seek the transcendent.”", "Say: “Augustine knew this from experience. His restless heart tried everything. Notice what it implies: if we have a hunger that nothing in this world fully satisfies, maybe we were made for more than this world. C. S. Lewis made a similar argument: ‘If I find in myself a desire which no experience in this world can satisfy, the most probable explanation is that I was made for another world.’ Hunger points to food, thirst to water. The desire for God points to God.”", "Say: “This desire can be pushed down. We can fill our days with noise, entertainment and distraction, which Pascal called ‘diversion.’ Some people turn away from God. Others focus on pleasure, power or possessions. But the desire returns. Faith is the answer to this desire, but God first reaches out to us. In Revelation, Jesus says, ‘Behold, I stand at the door and knock.’ The call to seek God is a call to listen to our deepest longings, ask honest questions, and pray. As Jesus said, ‘Seek and you will find.’ (Matthew 7:7)”"],
    ask=["What does the Catechism say about the desire for God? (It is written in the human heart because we were made by God and for God.)", "What did Augustine mean by a restless heart? (Nothing but God satisfies our deepest longing.)", "What is Pascal’s idea of diversion? (We fill our lives with distractions to avoid facing the big questions.)"],
    practice=["Make a chart with three columns: What I want, How long it satisfied me, What I wanted next.", "Fill in five wants.", "Look for a pattern. Write a sentence about what your wants seem to be reaching for.", "Rewrite Augustine’s line in your own words as a prayer."],
    close="Pray Augustine’s line quietly: “You have made us for yourself, O Lord, and our heart is restless until it rests in you.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Notice what you reach for when you are bored or lonely.”")),

'9-1-2': dict(
  saint="Anselm of Canterbury: a monk who defined theology as faith seeking understanding.",
  tryit=["Lay out one argument for God’s existence step by step.", "Name the premise a skeptic might challenge.", "Write a reply."],
  saintStory="Anselm was born around 1033 in Aosta, in northern Italy. After a difficult childhood, he left home, wandered through France and joined the Benedictine abbey of Bec in Normandy. He became prior, then abbot. His students loved him. He was gentle and wise, but also a brilliant thinker.\nOne day he wondered whether there might be a single argument that would prove God’s existence. He had trouble eating and sleeping over the problem. Then, he said, it came to him, and he wrote the Proslogion. His motto was “faith seeking understanding” (fides quaerens intellectum): he did not try to reason in order to believe, but believed and wanted to understand more.\nIn 1093, he was reluctantly made archbishop of Canterbury. He clashed with two English kings, William Rufus and Henry I, over the Church’s freedom, and twice went into exile. He died in 1109 and is a Doctor of the Church.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Can you prove that your best friend exists? Not in a court, but to someone who has never met them? What kind of proof would count?” Take answers. “Proof comes in different kinds.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Church teaches that God can be known by reason from the created world. The First Vatican Council taught that ‘God can be known with certainty from created things by the natural light of human reason.’ (CCC 36) Paul wrote that ‘ever since the creation of the world his invisible nature… has been clearly perceived in the things that have been made.’ (Romans 1:20) Reason cannot reach everything about God, such as the Trinity, but it can show that he exists and some of what he is like.”", "Say: “Thomas Aquinas gave five ways. One: motion. Things move, and each thing is moved by another, but this cannot go back forever; there must be a first mover that is not moved. Two: cause. Nothing causes itself; there must be a first cause. Three: contingency. Things exist that might not have; there must be something that exists necessarily. Four: degrees of perfection. We see that things are more or less good and true; there must be something that is perfectly good and true. Five: order. Things in nature act toward ends, like an arrow shot by an archer; there must be an intelligence that directs them. We call this God. The Catechism lists other paths: the world and the human person.”", "Say: “Anselm gave a different argument. God is ‘that than which nothing greater can be thought.’ Even a person who denies God understands this idea. But something that exists in reality is greater than something that exists only in the mind. Therefore God must exist in reality. Philosophers have debated it for centuries; many find it puzzling. These arguments do not force faith, but they show that belief in God is reasonable. They clear the way. The step from ‘a first cause exists’ to ‘Jesus is Lord’ requires revelation and grace. The arguments are like a road, but faith is arriving at a person.”"],
    ask=["Can reason know God exists? (Yes, the Church teaches it can, from created things.)", "Name two of Aquinas’s five ways. (Motion, cause, contingency, degrees, design.)", "What does ‘faith seeking understanding’ mean? (Believing, and then wanting to understand more.)"],
    practice=["Choose one of Aquinas’s five ways, such as the argument from cause.", "Write it as numbered steps: premise 1, premise 2, conclusion.", "Write the strongest objection a skeptic could raise.", "Write a reply, then swap with a partner and critique."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, I do not seek to understand so that I may believe, but I believe so that I may understand.” (Anselm.) Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Explain one argument for God to someone.”")),

'9-1-3': dict(
  saint="Thérèse of Lisieux: a young nun who kept believing in the dark.",
  tryit=["Sort reasons for faith into evidence, trust and choice.", "Mark what you can check and what you must decide.", "Write what makes faith free."],
  saintStory="Thérèse spent nearly all her short life in faith without any struggle. She had been a joyful child and wrote about God with simple confidence. But in April 1896, the year before she died, she began to have a severe trial of faith. Suddenly the thought of heaven, which had been so sweet, was blocked by a thick fog. She wrote that she heard a voice mocking her: heaven does not exist, there is nothing after death.\nShe did not stop praying. She wrote that she made constant acts of faith, even when she felt nothing. She said she was sitting at the table of sinners and unbelievers, eating their bread of sorrow, offering it for them. In her last weeks, suffering from tuberculosis, she kept going, saying, “I have never given the good God anything but love.”\nShe died on September 30, 1897. Her doubts were not a lack of faith but a place where faith was tested. She is a Doctor of the Church.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Is faith the same as a guess? Is it believing something with no evidence? Or is it more like trusting a friend?” Take answers. Let several disagree.",
    teach=["Say: “The Church teaches that faith is both reasonable and free. Faith is a gift of God, a grace, and it is also a human act. The Catechism says ‘Believing is possible only by grace and the interior helps of the Holy Spirit. But it is no less true that believing is an authentically human act.’ (CCC 154) Faith is not contrary to reason. John Paul II began his encyclical Fides et Ratio, ‘Faith and reason are like two wings on which the human spirit rises to the contemplation of truth.’ A bird needs two wings.”", "Say: “What are the reasons for faith? God’s revelation is accompanied by ‘proofs’: miracles, prophecies fulfilled, the holiness and growth of the Church, her stability, and above all the Resurrection of Jesus. These are called motives of credibility. They do not make faith a conclusion of logic, but show that it is reasonable to trust. Then, because the truths of faith go beyond what we see, we trust God’s word. We believe, not because we understand, but because God, who cannot deceive, has spoken. Faith is a trust, like trusting a doctor or a friend.”", "Say: “Faith is also free. God never forces. Jesus said, ‘If any man would come after me…’ The Church teaches the right to religious freedom: no one should be forced to believe, because faith must be a free response. Faith also grows. It can be tested, as Thérèse’s was. Doubt is not the same as unbelief; the father in Mark 9:24 said, ‘I believe; help my unbelief!’ Honest questions are welcome; Thomas questioned and was answered. Persistent, willful denial is different. How do we grow in faith? Pray, read Scripture, receive the sacraments, ask questions, find good friends and serve others.”"],
    ask=["Is faith opposed to reason? (No; they are two wings.)", "What are motives of credibility? (Signs and reasons that make faith reasonable.)", "Is doubt a sin? (Honest doubt is not; willful rejection is different.)"],
    practice=["Draw a table: Reasons for faith / Trust in God / My free choice.", "Under each, list items: the Resurrection and witnesses, the Church’s history and saints, my experience of prayer, a decision to keep going.", "Mark what you can check and what you must decide.", "Write one honest question you have and who you could ask."],
    close="Pray: “I believe; help my unbelief.” (Mark 9:24.) Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Ask a trusted adult a question you have about faith.”")),

'9-2-1': dict(
  saint="John of the Cross: a Carmelite who wrote poems from a prison cell about God’s approach to the soul.",
  tryit=["Trace the stages of revelation on a timeline.", "Name who God spoke through at each.", "Mark the fullness."],
  saintStory="John was born in 1542 in Fontiveros, Spain, to a poor family; his father died when he was young. He worked in a hospital, and then entered the Carmelites. He met Teresa of Avila, who asked him to help her reform the order, and he became a leader of the “discalced,” or barefoot, Carmelites.\nOpponents in the unreformed order kidnapped him in December 1577 and locked him in a tiny cell in Toledo for nine months. It was dark, hot in summer and freezing in winter. He was beaten and given little food. In that cell, he composed some of the great poems in the Spanish language, including part of the Spiritual Canticle. He escaped through a window in August 1578.\nHe wrote The Ascent of Mount Carmel and The Dark Night of the Soul. He taught that God reveals himself fully in Christ: “In giving us his Son, his only Word, God spoke everything to us at once in this sole Word, and he has no more to say.” He died in 1591 at forty-nine.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “How do we get to know a person? Can someone know you by looking at you? What does it take for someone to truly know you?” Take answers. “Someone has to tell you, and you have to tell them. Revelation is God telling us who he is.”",
    teach=["Say: “Revelation means God revealing himself and his plan. We cannot climb up to God; he has to come to us. The Catechism says: ‘God, who “dwells in unapproachable light”, wants to communicate his own divine life to the men he freely created.’ (CCC 52) He does it in stages, ‘by deeds and words,’ in history. This is called the economy of revelation, or salvation history. The Bible is the record, but the revelation is mainly God’s action and word in history.”", "Say: “The stages. One: in creation, God reveals himself through the world. Two: to Adam and Eve, God gave a promise after the fall, the first Gospel (Genesis 3:15). Three: to Noah, a covenant with all creation. Four: to Abraham, who was called to be the father of a people. Five: to Moses and Israel, at the Exodus and Sinai, God revealed his name and gave the Law. Six: through the prophets, who called Israel back and gave hope of a new covenant. Seven: in Jesus Christ, the Word made flesh. ‘In many and various ways God spoke of old to our fathers by the prophets; but in these last days he has spoken to us by a Son.’ (Hebrews 1:1-2)”", "Say: “Christ is the fullness of revelation. There will be no new public revelation after him and the apostles. The Church speaks of the end of public revelation with the death of the last apostle. This doesn’t mean that nothing new is learned: the Church grows in understanding. Private revelations, such as apparitions at Lourdes or Fatima, do not add to the faith, but help people live it. The Church may approve them as worthy of belief but never requires belief. God also reveals himself in a personal way in prayer. As John of the Cross said, Christ is the whole of God’s word.”"],
    ask=["What is revelation? (God revealing himself and his plan.)", "Who is the fullness of revelation? (Jesus Christ.)", "Do private revelations add to the faith? (No; they help people live the faith.)"],
    practice=["Draw a timeline from creation to Christ with seven stops.", "Under each, write the person or event, and what God revealed about himself.", "Circle where it reaches its fullness.", "Write a sentence: “God revealed himself gradually because…”"],
    close="Pray the opening of Hebrews 1: “In many and various ways God spoke of old.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Read Hebrews 1:1-4 aloud.”")),

'9-2-2': dict(
  saint="Vincent of Lérins: a monk who asked how we know what is truly Catholic.",
  tryit=["Apply Vincent’s test to three doctrines.", "Find where they appear in Scripture and the Fathers.", "Say what is Tradition and what is only custom."],
  saintStory="Vincent was a monk on the island of Lérins, off the coast of southern France, in the fifth century. The monastery was a center of study and prayer; Honoratus founded it, and many bishops trained there. Vincent had been a soldier and a man of the world before becoming a monk.\nIn about 434 he wrote a short book, the Commonitory, under the name Peregrinus (“pilgrim”), for those who wanted help in recognizing the true faith amid many teachers. His rule: hold “what has been believed everywhere, always, and by all.” He also wrote that the faith can grow, as a body grows from child to adult, without changing its nature: “Let there be progress, but let it be real progress, not change of faith.”\nWe know little else about him. He died before 450. His simple test is still quoted in Church documents.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “If two people tell you different versions of a family story, how do you decide which is right? What would you look for?” Take answers. “The Church faces this question about the faith itself.”",
    teach=["Say: “God’s revelation is handed on in two ways, called Sacred Tradition and Sacred Scripture. Vatican II’s Dei Verbum says they ‘flow from the same divine wellspring’ and ‘merge into a unity and tend toward the same end.’ (DV 9) Sacred Scripture is the word of God written down under the Holy Spirit. Sacred Tradition transmits the word of God, entrusted to the apostles by Christ and the Holy Spirit, to their successors, so that they may preserve, expound and spread it. The Church accepts both with equal devotion and reverence.”", "Say: “Some Christians say ‘Scripture alone’ (sola scriptura). Catholics respond: where does the Bible say that? Paul wrote, ‘Stand firm and hold to the traditions which you were taught by us, either by word of mouth or by letter.’ (2 Thessalonians 2:15) John wrote that Jesus did many other things not written (John 21:25). The Church existed before the New Testament was written, and it was the Church that determined which books belong in the Bible. Tradition tells us what books are Scripture, and how to read them. Examples of Tradition not explicit in Scripture: the canon of the Bible, Sunday worship, infant Baptism, the Trinity’s terminology.”", "Say: “Tradition (capital T) is not the same as traditions (small t). Capital T Tradition is the apostolic teaching, which cannot change. Small t traditions are customs, devotions and disciplines that vary by time and place, like the Rosary, the Latin Mass, or fasting practices; they can change. Vincent’s test helps to tell the difference: universality (everywhere), antiquity (always), consensus (by all). Today, the Church’s teaching authority, the Magisterium, interprets both. The three work together. Without Tradition, the Bible is read without context; without Scripture, Tradition would drift.”"],
    ask=["How is revelation handed on? (Through Sacred Scripture and Sacred Tradition.)", "What is the difference between Tradition and traditions? (Apostolic teaching vs. changeable customs.)", "What is Vincent’s test? (What has been believed everywhere, always, by all.)"],
    practice=["Make a list of items: the Trinity, Sunday Mass, the Rosary, infant Baptism, meatless Fridays in Lent, the sign of peace, the Assumption.", "Sort into Tradition (apostolic teaching) and traditions (customs).", "For each Tradition, find a source in Scripture and in the early Church (use the Catechism’s index).", "Write one sentence explaining your sorting."],
    close="Pray the Creed. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Ask someone where their faith traditions came from.”")),

'9-2-3': dict(
  saint="Robert Bellarmine: a Jesuit cardinal who explained the Church’s authority with patience and clarity.",
  tryit=["Sort teachings into infallible and non-infallible.", "Say who can teach each.", "Write what it asks of us."],
  saintStory="Robert Bellarmine was born in 1542 in Montepulciano, Italy, to a family related to Pope Marcellus II. He joined the Jesuits at eighteen, against his father’s wishes. He was small and bright, a gifted preacher, and became a professor at Louvain and Rome. He wrote Disputations on the Controversies of the Christian Faith, a clear, fair-minded defense of Catholic teaching against Protestant arguments, so careful that even Protestants cited it. He also wrote a catechism used for centuries.\nHe was made a cardinal in 1599, and served as a Vatican adviser. In 1616, he was the one who delivered to Galileo the Church’s warning about teaching the Sun-centered system as established fact. Bellarmine said that if there were a real proof, the Church would need to reinterpret the Scriptures. But there was no proof yet.\nHe lived simply as a cardinal and gave his money to the poor, and died in 1621. He is a Doctor of the Church.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Who decides the rules of a game when there is a disagreement? Why must there be a referee? What would happen without one?” Take answers. “Jesus knew the Church would need an authority.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Magisterium is the Church’s living teaching office: the Pope and the bishops in communion with him. Jesus gave this authority to the apostles: ‘He who hears you hears me.’ (Luke 10:16) ‘Whatever you bind on earth shall be bound in heaven.’ (Matthew 18:18) He promised that ‘the Spirit of truth will guide you into all the truth.’ (John 16:13) The Magisterium is not above the word of God, but serves it, ‘teaching only what has been handed on.’ (DV 10)”", "Say: “Catholics speak of infallibility. This means that, by the Holy Spirit’s assistance, the Church cannot err in teaching a matter of faith or morals when it is definitively proposed. It is exercised in three ways. One: the Pope speaks ex cathedra (‘from the chair’), as shepherd of all Christians, defining a doctrine on faith or morals to be held by the whole Church. This has been done rarely: for example, the Immaculate Conception (1854) and the Assumption (1950). Two: the college of bishops, in union with the Pope, in an ecumenical council, such as Nicaea or Vatican II. Three: the ordinary and universal Magisterium, when the bishops all over the world, in union with the Pope, agree on a teaching as definitive. Infallibility doesn’t mean the Pope is sinless or right about everything. He can be wrong about science, politics or personal opinions.”", "Say: “There are different levels of teaching. Dogmas must be believed by divine and Catholic faith. Definitive teachings must be firmly held. Authoritative non-definitive teaching, such as encyclicals and the Catechism, requires ‘religious submission of mind and will.’ (LG 25) Prudential judgments, such as how to handle a political problem, are not binding in the same way. Catholics are invited to study, ask questions and discuss. But the Church asks us to give a docile and trusting response. The Magisterium gives us stability. In a world of constant opinions, we know we are not left alone.”"],
    ask=["Who forms the Magisterium? (The Pope and the bishops in union with him.)", "Does infallibility mean the Pope is sinless? (No; it concerns defined teaching on faith and morals.)", "Give an example of an infallible definition. (The Immaculate Conception; the Assumption.)"],
    practice=["Prepare cards: Resurrection of Christ, the Assumption, an encyclical on the environment, the Pope’s opinion on a football team, the Nicene Creed, a bishop’s letter on a local issue, the Catechism.", "Sort by level of authority: dogma, definitive, authoritative teaching, opinion or prudential.", "Discuss one that surprised you.", "Write what each level asks of a Catholic."],
    close="Pray for the Pope and bishops. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Look up when the Assumption was defined.”")),

'9-3-1': dict(
  saint="Gregory the Great: a pope who called Scripture a letter from God to his creature.",
  tryit=["Read 2 Timothy 3:16 and Dei Verbum 11.", "Write what is taught without error.", "Explain how God and the human author both write."],
  saintStory="Gregory was born around 540 into a wealthy Roman senatorial family. He became prefect of Rome, then gave up his career, turned his house into a monastery and gave his fortune to the poor. He wanted a quiet monastic life, but the Church needed him. He was sent as an ambassador to Constantinople, and in 590 was elected pope, at a time when plague, floods and invasions threatened Rome.\nHe called himself “servant of the servants of God,” a title popes still use. He organized food for the poor, defended Rome, and sent Augustine and forty monks to England in 596. He wrote many sermons and letters on Scripture. In one, to a physician, he asked, “What is Scripture but a kind of letter from almighty God to his creature? If an earthly king sent you a letter, you would not rest until you had read it. The Emperor of heaven has sent you a letter for your life, and yet you neglect to read it.”\nHe died in 604. He is a Doctor of the Church, and Gregorian chant is named for him.",
  script=dict(
    open="Read aloud a passage from the Bible, but don’t say what it is. Then say, “Who wrote this? Several answers are correct.” Let students answer. “Today we make sense of that.”",
    teach=["Read 2 Timothy 3:16-17 and 2 Peter 1:20-21. Say: “All Scripture is ‘breathed out by God.’ The Church teaches that God is the author of Scripture. The Holy Spirit inspired human authors, who wrote as true authors, using their own faculties and abilities. Dei Verbum 11 says: ‘Everything asserted by the inspired authors or sacred writers must be held to be asserted by the Holy Spirit.’ So the Church holds that the books of Scripture ‘firmly, faithfully, and without error teach that truth which God, for the sake of our salvation, wished to see confided to the Sacred Scriptures.’”", "Say: “Notice the phrases. ‘Without error’ is called inerrancy. It applies to what the sacred authors intend to teach for our salvation, not to every detail of history or science. The Bible is not a science textbook, nor does it always give precise measures; it can use round numbers, symbolic language, and imprecise memory. If the Bible tells a parable, the truth is the lesson, not the facts of the story. What the author affirms is true. But we must know what the author affirms, which is why we study the literary forms and context.”", "Say: “Some people misunderstand inspiration in two directions. One thinks God dictated every word, like a secretary taking dictation, so human authors contribute nothing; this is fundamentalism. The other thinks the Bible is only human religious literature, with no divine authorship. The Catholic position is both: God is the principal author; the human authors are real authors. It parallels the Incarnation: Christ is fully divine and fully human; Scripture is God’s word in human words. Gregory’s idea of a letter helps: a letter from God, written through a person, addressed to each of us.”"],
    ask=["Who is the principal author of Scripture? (God, the Holy Spirit.)", "What does inerrancy cover? (What the sacred authors affirm for the sake of our salvation.)", "What is fundamentalism? (Reading every word as dictated and literal, ignoring human authorship and literary form.)"],
    practice=["Read Dei Verbum 11 together.", "Underline what it says about God and about human authors.", "Draw a diagram with God, the Holy Spirit, a human author, and the text, showing how they relate.", "Write a one-paragraph answer to: “Is the Bible without error?”"],
    close="Pray: “Lord, you have written me a letter. Help me to read it.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Read the Bible as a letter for a week.”")),

'9-3-2': dict(
  saint="Bonaventure: a Franciscan teacher who showed how Scripture speaks on many levels.",
  tryit=["Interpret one passage in four senses.", "Write each sense in a sentence.", "Compare with the literal."],
  saintStory="Bonaventure was born in 1221 in Bagnoregio, Italy. According to a family story, as a child he was gravely ill, and his mother prayed to Francis of Assisi, and he recovered. Bonaventure later joined the Franciscans and studied in Paris, where he taught alongside Thomas Aquinas. He was a brilliant theologian, but humble. Once, when two visitors from the pope came to deliver his cardinal’s hat, they found him washing dishes. He asked them to hang the hat on a tree until he was finished.\nIn 1257, he was elected minister general of the Franciscans, and he guided the order through a troubled period, writing the official life of Francis. He believed that studying should lead to prayer. His book, The Soul’s Journey into God, takes the soul step by step from creation to contemplation.\nHe commented on Scripture, finding in it a many-layered meaning. He was made a cardinal in 1273 and died in 1274 during the Council of Lyon. He is a Doctor of the Church, the “Seraphic Doctor.”",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a picture of an onion or layered cake and ask, “What does it mean to say the Bible has layers?” Then read Exodus 14 (the Red Sea) briefly, and ask, “What is this passage about? Is there more than one answer?”",
    teach=["Say: “Dei Verbum gives three rules for reading Scripture as it was written: be attentive to the literary forms; be attentive to the unity of the whole of Scripture; and read it within the living Tradition of the Church, guided by the analogy of faith. The first level of interpretation is the literal sense: the meaning intended by the human author, discovered by careful study of language, context, genre and history. All other senses are based on the literal. It would be wrong to build a spiritual meaning on a misunderstanding of what the text says.”", "Say: “The Church also speaks of the spiritual senses, which are meanings intended by God, the author, beyond the literal. There are three. The allegorical sense: we can see the significance of events by seeing how they point to Christ. The crossing of the Red Sea is a sign of Christ’s victory and of Baptism. The moral sense: events are written for our instruction, to lead us to act justly. ‘These things happened to them as a warning, but they were written down for our instruction.’ (1 Corinthians 10:11) The anagogical sense (from the Greek for ‘leading up’): events point to our eternal destiny. Jerusalem in the Bible is a sign of the heavenly city.”", "Say: “Let’s see them with the word ‘Jerusalem.’ Literally, it is a city in Palestine. Allegorically, it is the Church. Morally, it is the soul that is at peace with God. Anagogically, it is the heavenly Jerusalem. Notice that the New Testament itself reads the Old Testament this way. Paul in Galatians 4 reads Hagar and Sarah as allegory. Jesus says that Jonah is a sign of his burial. Not every passage has all four, and these senses can’t be used arbitrarily; they must agree with the faith of the Church. We also should be careful to avoid reading our own preferences into the text. The Church’s liturgy and the Fathers are good guides.”"],
    ask=["What are the two main kinds of sense in Scripture? (Literal and spiritual.)", "Name the three spiritual senses. (Allegorical, moral, anagogical.)", "What is the foundation of all senses? (The literal sense.)"],
    practice=["Choose a short passage, such as the Exodus crossing (Exodus 14:21-31) or Jonah in the fish.", "Write four sentences: what happened (literal), how it points to Christ or the Church (allegorical), how it teaches me to live (moral), and where it leads (anagogical).", "Check the Catechism or a Catholic Bible footnote for support.", "Share with a partner."],
    close="Pray the Collect from the Sunday Mass or: “Open our eyes to the wonders of your law.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Try the four senses on a Sunday reading.”")),

'9-3-3': dict(
  saint="Damasus I: the pope who asked Jerome for a Bible and helped fix the list of books.",
  tryit=["Read the canon lists and compare.", "Mark the seven disputed books.", "Explain who decides which books are Scripture."],
  saintStory="Damasus was born around 305, perhaps in Spain or Rome, and became pope in 366. His election was disputed by a rival, and rioting in Rome led to many deaths. Once secure, he worked to strengthen the Church’s unity and its identity. He restored the catacombs and the tombs of martyrs, and composed inscriptions in verse for them.\nIn 382, he held a council in Rome, which many scholars connect to a list of the books of Scripture. He also invited Jerome, the greatest biblical scholar of the day, to Rome as his secretary, and asked him to produce a standard Latin Bible by revising the Old Latin translations. This became the Vulgate.\nDamasus helped establish the place of the Roman see as the leading church, using the phrase “apostolic see.” He died in 384. His support for Jerome’s work shaped how the Church read the Bible for a thousand years.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “How did the Church decide which books go in the Bible? Who decided? When? What would you look for?” Write the criteria students propose on the board.",
    teach=["Say: “The canon is the official list of books that make up the Bible. The word means ‘rule’ or ‘measure.’ The Catholic canon has 73 books: 46 in the Old Testament and 27 in the New. The Church did not invent the canon, but discerned which books were inspired by the Holy Spirit. She used criteria: apostolic origin or connection; use in the liturgy and teaching of the churches; agreement with the rule of faith; and wide acceptance. By the late second century, most of the New Testament was in common use. Four Gospels were established early: Irenaeus said there could be neither more nor fewer than four.”", "Say: “The first list with exactly our 27 New Testament books is from Athanasius in 367. Local councils at Hippo (393) and Carthage (397, 419) approved the list, with the Pope’s approval. The Council of Trent in 1546 formally defined the canon, in response to Reformers who had rejected some books. The seven Old Testament books that Protestants omit (Tobit, Judith, Wisdom, Sirach, Baruch, 1 and 2 Maccabees, plus parts of Esther and Daniel) are called deuterocanonical, ‘second canon.’ They were in the Greek Septuagint, the Bible used by Jesus’ contemporaries and the early Church. Martin Luther removed them from the Old Testament because they were not in the later Hebrew Bible, which was fixed by the Jewish rabbis around AD 90-100, after Christianity had begun.”", "Say: “There were also writings that the Church rejected, often called apocrypha, such as the Gospel of Thomas, the Gospel of Judas and the Gospel of Peter. These were written in the second century or later, often under apostles’ names, and contain ideas that conflict with apostolic teaching (for example, gnostic ideas about matter being evil). The Church’s test was whether they were handed on from the apostles. The Da Vinci Code story, that the Council of Nicaea decided the Bible, is false; Nicaea did not discuss the canon. The canon shows that the Bible does not stand alone: the Church, guided by the Holy Spirit, recognized the inspired books. Scripture and the Church’s authority are linked.”"],
    ask=["How many books are in the Catholic Bible? (73.)", "Who defined the canon formally? (The Council of Trent in 1546, after earlier councils and lists.)", "What are deuterocanonical books? (Old Testament books in the Greek Septuagint not in the Hebrew Bible.)"],
    practice=["Give students a chart of the books of the Bible in a Catholic and in a Protestant canon.", "Highlight the differences.", "Write the criteria the Church used.", "Write a three-sentence answer for a friend who asks: “How do you know the Bible books are the right ones?”"],
    close="Pray: “Lord, thank you for the gift of the Bible.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Look up the deuterocanonical books in your Bible.”")),

'9-4-1': dict(
  saint="Melito of Sardis: a bishop who traveled to the Holy Land to learn the books of the Old Testament.",
  tryit=["Match Old Testament and New Testament pairs.", "Explain how the New fulfills the Old.", "Say why the Church keeps both."],
  saintStory="Melito was bishop of Sardis, in what is now Turkey, in the second century. He was widely respected and wrote many works, but most are lost. In one, he mentions that he traveled to the East, to the place where the Scriptures were preached and where the events happened, in order to learn accurately the books of the Old Testament. He gave a list of them, the earliest Christian list we have, which includes all the books of the Hebrew Bible except Esther.\nHis surviving work is a Paschal Homily, On Pascha, a great Easter sermon. In it he pictures the Old Testament as a sketch, a model that the artist makes before the final statue. When the real thing is complete, the model is no longer needed, but it had its role. “The model was precious before the reality, and the parable was wonderful before its interpretation. But when the church arose and the Savior appeared, the model was emptied, having yielded its power to the reality.”\nHe died in about 180.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show an architect’s model or blueprint and a picture of a finished building. Ask, “Is the blueprint false once the building is made? Is the building possible without it?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “Some early heretics, like Marcion in the second century, said the Old Testament God was an angry, lesser god, and that Christians should throw it away. The Church rejected this. The Old Testament is the word of God and is part of the Bible. The same God of the Old Testament is the Father of Jesus. The Catechism says: ‘The Old Testament is an indispensable part of Sacred Scripture. Its books are divinely inspired and retain a permanent value.’ (CCC 121) Jesus himself prayed the psalms and read the prophets. We call them one story in two parts.”", "Say: “Augustine’s phrase is helpful: ‘The New Testament is hidden in the Old; the Old is made manifest in the New.’ The Old Testament prepares for Christ. It reveals God’s plan, shows human need for salvation, and teaches about God, prayer and justice. The New Testament fulfills it. It does not abolish it. Jesus said, ‘I have come not to abolish but to fulfill.’ Examples: Adam and Christ (Romans 5), the Passover lamb and the Lamb of God, manna and the Eucharist, the Temple and Jesus’ body, the old covenant and the new covenant.”", "Say: “Some say the Old Testament is violent. It does include difficult passages: wars, harsh laws. The Church teaches that revelation is gradual and that God was educating a people. Some passages reflect the ancient world’s values and are not an endorsement of violence. We read them in the light of Christ, who is the fullness. And the Jewish people remain beloved by God: Paul wrote, ‘The gifts and the call of God are irrevocable.’ (Romans 11:29) The Church condemns antisemitism, and recognizes the Jewish people as our ‘elder brothers’ (John Paul II). The covenant with Israel has not been revoked.”"],
    ask=["Why does the Church keep the Old Testament? (It is God’s inspired word and prepares for Christ.)", "What did Marcion teach? (That the Old Testament should be rejected; the Church condemned it.)", "How does the New fulfill the Old? (It completes and reveals the meaning of what was promised.)"],
    practice=["Make a matching chart: Old Testament item / New Testament fulfillment.", "Include: Adam / Christ; Passover lamb / Lamb of God; manna / Eucharist; Jonah / Resurrection; Temple / Christ’s body; Melchizedek / Christ the priest.", "Add the verses.", "Write a sentence explaining why we need both Testaments."],
    close="Pray Psalm 119:105: “Your word is a lamp to my feet.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Read an Old Testament passage and look for Jesus.”")),

'9-4-2': dict(
  saint="Ruth: a foreign widow whose loyalty made her the great-grandmother of David.",
  tryit=["Trace the covenants on a chart.", "Mark each one’s sign and promise.", "Write how Christ completes them."],
  saintStory="Ruth was a Moabite woman, from a people who were Israel’s enemies. She married an Israelite man who had come to Moab with his family during a famine. Her husband died, as did her husband’s brother and father, leaving three widows: Naomi, Ruth and Orpah. Naomi decided to return to Bethlehem, and urged her daughters-in-law to go home. Orpah went back. Ruth said, “Do not urge me to leave you or to return from following you; for where you go I will go, and where you lodge I will lodge; your people shall be my people, and your God my God.”\nIn Bethlehem, Ruth gleaned grain in the fields, and met Boaz, a relative of Naomi’s husband, who treated her with great kindness. He acted as her kinsman-redeemer and married her. Their son Obed was the father of Jesse, the father of David. So Ruth, a foreigner, became an ancestor of David and of Christ. She appears in Matthew’s genealogy.\nHer story shows hesed, covenant loyalty, which is a theme of God’s dealing with Israel.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “What is the difference between a contract and a promise made in love? What is a marriage? Is it a contract? A covenant? Why do people use that word?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “A covenant is a sacred bond that establishes a family relationship, with promises and signs, sealed with an oath or a ritual. It is more than a contract. A contract exchanges goods and services; a covenant exchanges persons: ‘I will be your God, and you shall be my people.’ The Bible is the story of God’s covenants, each a step in the plan. They show God’s fidelity: he keeps his word even when we break ours.”", "Say: “The covenants: With Adam, the covenant of creation, in which God made man in his image (Genesis 1-2). With Noah, God promised never again to destroy the earth by flood; the sign is the rainbow. With Abraham, God promised land, descendants and blessing to all nations; the sign is circumcision. With Moses at Sinai, God gave the Law, and Israel became a priestly kingdom and holy nation; the sign is the Sabbath. With David, God promised that a son of David would reign forever (2 Samuel 7), the promise of the Messiah. And the new covenant, promised by Jeremiah, which is fulfilled by Christ.”", "Say: “In each covenant, God makes the first move, and the people often fail. The prophets denounce Israel’s unfaithfulness as adultery. Yet God does not abandon. Hosea shows a husband who keeps loving a faithless wife. In Jeremiah 31, God promises a new covenant, with a law written on hearts, and forgiveness of sins. At the Last Supper, Jesus said, ‘This cup is the new covenant in my blood.’ The Eucharist makes us members of the covenant. Baptism is the sign. The new covenant doesn’t cancel the older covenants but completes them. And God’s covenant with the Jewish people is still valid.”"],
    ask=["What is a covenant? (A sacred bond that makes a family relationship.)", "What was the sign of the covenant with Noah? (The rainbow.)", "What is the new covenant? (Established in Christ’s blood, with a law written on hearts.)"],
    practice=["Draw a table with six rows: Adam, Noah, Abraham, Moses, David, Christ.", "Fill in columns: the promise, the sign, what the people failed to do, what God did.", "Under the last row, write how Christ completes the others.", "Write a short summary: “God’s covenant love shows that…”"],
    close="Pray: “Lord, you are faithful; make us faithful.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Read the book of Ruth; it’s four short chapters.”")),

'9-4-3': dict(
  saint="Anna the prophetess: an old widow who recognized the child in the Temple.",
  tryit=["Chart ten prophecies and fulfillments.", "Rate each by how specific it is.", "Explain why the pattern matters."],
  saintStory="Anna was a prophetess, the daughter of Phanuel, of the tribe of Asher. She was eighty-four years old, and had been married only seven years before she was widowed. Since then, she lived in the Temple in Jerusalem, “worshiping with fasting and prayer night and day.”\nWhen Mary and Joseph brought the infant Jesus to the Temple to present him to the Lord, as the Law required, the old man Simeon took the baby in his arms and blessed God: “My eyes have seen your salvation, which you have prepared in the presence of all peoples.” At that very hour, Anna came up, gave thanks to God, and spoke about the child to all who were looking for the redemption of Jerusalem.\nSimeon and Anna are the last figures of Old Testament waiting. After long hope, they saw the promise come true. Luke places her at the opening of the Gospel to show that prayer and fasting prepared Israel for the Messiah.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “If someone made a prediction years ago that came true in detail, how would you react? What if there were dozens?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “The Old Testament is full of promises that look forward. The Catechism says that Christians read the Old Testament ‘in the light of Christ crucified and risen.’ (CCC 129) Prophecy is not just predicting the future; the prophets also called Israel to faithfulness. But they also gave promises of a Messiah, and the New Testament shows Jesus as their fulfillment. Jesus himself said so: ‘Everything written about me in the law of Moses and the prophets and the psalms must be fulfilled.’ (Luke 24:44)”", "Say: “Some prophecies. Born of a virgin: Isaiah 7:14 / Matthew 1:23. Born in Bethlehem: Micah 5:2 / Matthew 2:1-6. Called out of Egypt: Hosea 11:1 / Matthew 2:15. A herald in the wilderness: Isaiah 40:3 / Mark 1:2-4. A king on a donkey: Zechariah 9:9 / Matthew 21:5. Betrayed for thirty pieces of silver: Zechariah 11:12-13 / Matthew 26:15. Pierced hands and feet, garments divided: Psalm 22 / John 19. A suffering servant who bears our sins: Isaiah 53 / 1 Peter 2:24. Not one bone broken: Psalm 34:20 / John 19:36. A Son of Man who comes with the clouds: Daniel 7 / Mark 14:62.”", "Say: “How do we evaluate this? Some objections: Jesus’ followers may have shaped the story to fit. But some things, like birthplace or crucifixion, can’t be arranged. Some prophecies had a first meaning in their own time and a deeper meaning later (a ‘fuller sense’). The point is the pattern: the whole Old Testament leads toward Christ in many ways, like pieces of a puzzle that fit. The convergence is the strongest evidence, not one single verse. And we should be careful not to treat the Old Testament as only prediction: it has its own meaning. Together they give a coherent story that God has been preparing. The fulfillment invites faith, but doesn’t force it.”"],
    ask=["What did Jesus say about the Old Testament? (It was written about him and must be fulfilled.)", "Name one prophecy and fulfillment. (Bethlehem; virgin birth; suffering servant.)", "Why does the pattern matter? (Many pieces converge on Christ.)"],
    practice=["Build a chart of ten prophecies with their Old Testament and New Testament verses.", "Beside each, rate it: very specific, somewhat specific, or general.", "Choose the two you find strongest and explain why.", "Write a short reply to a friend who says the Gospel writers made it up."],
    close="Pray the Nunc Dimittis: “Lord, now let your servant depart in peace.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Learn Simeon’s prayer; the Church says it every night.”")),

'9-5-1': dict(
  saint="Matthew: a tax collector who left his table to follow Jesus.",
  tryit=["Collect sources outside the Bible that mention Jesus.", "Mark what each says.", "State what historians agree on."],
  saintStory="Matthew, also called Levi, was a tax collector in Capernaum, collecting taxes for the Romans and Herod Antipas. Tax collectors were hated as traitors and cheats. One day, Jesus walked by Matthew’s booth and said, “Follow me.” Matthew got up and followed him. That evening, he threw a banquet at his house for Jesus and many other tax collectors and sinners. The Pharisees complained. Jesus replied, “Those who are well have no need of a physician, but those who are sick. I came not to call the righteous, but sinners.”\nMatthew was one of the Twelve. Early Christians wrote that he composed a Gospel, first for Jewish Christians, and that he preached in Judea before going to other lands. We don’t know for certain how he died.\nHis background as a tax collector, used to keeping records, may explain why his Gospel is so organized. He gathers Jesus’ teaching into five great discourses, like the five books of Moses.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “How do we know that Julius Caesar existed? Or Socrates? What kinds of evidence do historians use?” Take answers: writings, archaeology, multiple independent sources. “Let’s ask the same about Jesus.”",
    teach=["Say: “The vast majority of historians, including non-Christians, agree that Jesus of Nazareth was a real person. Outside the Bible, we have mentions. Tacitus, a Roman historian, around AD 116, wrote that ‘Christus’ was executed by Pontius Pilate under Tiberius, and that his followers in Rome were persecuted by Nero. Josephus, a Jewish historian, around AD 93, mentioned Jesus and James, his brother, and John the Baptist (some of the longer passage was probably edited by Christians, but a core is original). Pliny the Younger wrote to Emperor Trajan around AD 112 about Christians who sang hymns to Christ ‘as to a god.’ Suetonius and the Jewish Talmud also mention him. Not a single ancient source denies he existed.”", "Say: “The Gospels are our main source. They were written within 40 to 70 years of the events, which is early for ancient history, by people who knew eyewitnesses. Paul’s letters, written in the 50s, describe Jesus’ death, Resurrection and the Last Supper, and he met Peter and James. We have thousands of manuscripts: over 5,800 Greek manuscripts of the New Testament, some from the second century, which is far more than any other ancient work. The Gospels include embarrassing details that the writers would not invent: Peter denying Jesus, the disciples fleeing, a crucified Messiah, women as first witnesses.”", "Say: “What do historians generally accept? Jesus was baptized by John. He preached the kingdom of God, taught in parables, and performed healings and exorcisms that people attributed to him. He gathered disciples, including twelve. He was crucified under Pontius Pilate. Soon after, his followers claimed he was risen from the dead, and many suffered for it. Historians disagree about the meaning, but the Church’s faith is rooted in history. As the Catechism says, we believe in a real person who lived at a particular time and place. Luke wrote, ‘It seemed good to me also, having followed all things closely for some time past, to write an orderly account.’”"],
    ask=["Name one non-Christian source for Jesus. (Tacitus, Josephus, Pliny.)", "How many Greek New Testament manuscripts survive? (Over 5,800.)", "What embarrassing details support the Gospels’ reliability? (Peter’s denial, the disciples fleeing, women as first witnesses.)"],
    practice=["Make a chart: Source, Date, What it says about Jesus.", "Include Tacitus, Josephus, Pliny, Paul and the Gospels.", "Add a column: Independent of Christian sources?", "Write a three-sentence summary of what we can know about Jesus from history."],
    close="Pray: “Lord Jesus, you are real and present. Help me to follow you.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Read Tacitus’s mention of Christ online with a parent.”")),

'9-5-2': dict(
  saint="Cyril of Alexandria: a bishop who defended Mary as Theotokos, Mother of God.",
  tryit=["Match each heresy to its error and the Church’s answer.", "Mark what Chalcedon said.", "Explain the doctrine in your own words."],
  saintStory="Cyril became patriarch of Alexandria in 412. He was a strong, sometimes fierce leader, and an outstanding theologian. In 428, Nestorius, the patriarch of Constantinople, objected to calling Mary Theotokos, “God-bearer” or Mother of God, saying she should be called Christotokos, “Christ-bearer,” because she was the mother of Christ’s human nature only. Cyril saw that this split Christ into two persons.\nHe wrote to Nestorius, and appealed to Pope Celestine, who backed him. In 431, the Council of Ephesus met. Cyril presided for the Pope. The council affirmed that Jesus is one Person, the Son of God, and that Mary is the Mother of God, not because she is the source of his divinity but because the child she bore is God.\nWhen the news reached Ephesus, the people reportedly filled the streets with torchlight celebrations. Cyril died in 444. He is a Doctor of the Church.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “What do you do when you hold two things that seem to contradict? How can someone be both a child and an adult, or both king and servant?” Take answers. “That’s the puzzle of Jesus.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Church believes that Jesus is true God and true man. The first Christians believed it from the start (John 1:1, 14; Philippians 2:6-8; Colossians 2:9), but it took centuries to say it clearly, as errors arose. The heresies are mirror errors. Docetism: Jesus only seemed to be human. Arianism: Jesus is not fully God but a created being; condemned at Nicaea in 325. Apollinarianism: Jesus had no human mind. Nestorianism: Jesus is two persons, a divine and a human, joined together; condemned at Ephesus in 431. Monophysitism: Jesus’ human nature was absorbed into the divine, so he has only one nature; condemned at Chalcedon in 451.”", "Say: “Chalcedon taught: Jesus is ‘truly God and truly man, consisting of a rational soul and a body; consubstantial with the Father in his divinity, and consubstantial with us in his humanity; like us in all things except sin.’ He is one Person in two natures, ‘without confusion, without change, without division, without separation.’ Later, the Third Council of Constantinople in 681 added that he has two wills, divine and human, which are in harmony. Jesus’ human will freely follows his divine will, as in Gethsemane.”", "Say: “Why does this matter? If Jesus is not fully God, he can’t save us or reveal God. If he is not fully man, he can’t represent us or truly die for us. Gregory of Nazianzus said, ‘What is not assumed is not healed.’ Because Jesus is one Person, we can say that God was born, God suffered and God died. This is why Mary is Theotokos: the child she bore is God. It’s about Jesus, not just Mary. We should not think of Jesus as God pretending to be man, or as a man with divine help. He is both, entirely. We pray to him as a divine Person, and he knows our life from within.”"],
    ask=["What does Chalcedon say about Jesus? (One Person in two natures, divine and human.)", "Why is Mary called Theotokos? (The child she bore is God.)", "What did Nestorius teach? (That Jesus is two persons.)"],
    practice=["Prepare a chart: Heresy, What it taught, What it denied, Church’s response.", "Include Docetism, Arianism, Apollinarianism, Nestorianism, Monophysitism.", "Rewrite the Chalcedon formula in plain English.", "Write a short explanation for a friend: “Why does it matter that Jesus is fully God and fully man?”"],
    close="Pray the Hail Mary, pausing at “Mother of God.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Explain to someone why Mary is Mother of God.”")),

'9-5-3': dict(
  saint="Thomas the apostle: the doubter who made the greatest confession of faith.",
  tryit=["List Jesus’ “I am” statements.", "Mark which claim divinity.", "Explain why Lewis calls the choice Lord, liar or lunatic."],
  saintStory="Thomas, also called Didymus, “the twin,” was one of the Twelve. John’s Gospel tells us the most about him. When Jesus decided to go to Bethany, near Jerusalem, where he had enemies, to raise Lazarus, Thomas said to the others, “Let us also go, that we may die with him.” At the Last Supper, when Jesus said, “You know the way where I am going,” Thomas said, “Lord, we do not know where you are going; how can we know the way?” Jesus answered, “I am the way, and the truth, and the life.”\nAfter the Resurrection, Thomas was not with the others when Jesus appeared. He said, “Unless I see in his hands the print of the nails, and place my finger in the mark of the nails, and place my hand in his side, I will not believe.” A week later, Jesus came again and said, “Put your finger here, and see my hands; and put out your hand, and place it in my side; do not be faithless, but believing.” Thomas answered, “My Lord and my God!”\nTradition says Thomas carried the Gospel to India, where he was martyred near Chennai, around AD 72. The Saint Thomas Christians in Kerala trace their faith to him.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “If someone said, ‘I am God,’ what would you think? What would you want to see?” Take answers. “Jesus made claims that people couldn’t ignore.”",
    teach=["Say: “Jesus did not call himself ‘God’ in so many words in the Gospels, but his claims point to it. He spoke with authority: ‘You have heard that it was said… but I say to you.’ He forgave sins: when he told the paralytic, ‘Your sins are forgiven,’ the scribes said, ‘Who can forgive sins but God alone?’ (Mark 2:7) He said, ‘Before Abraham was, I am.’ (John 8:58) The listeners picked up stones, since they understood he was using God’s name. He said, ‘I and the Father are one.’ (John 10:30). They tried to stone him for blasphemy.”", "Say: “John’s Gospel has seven ‘I am’ statements: bread of life, light of the world, the door, the good shepherd, the resurrection and the life, the way, truth and life, the true vine. At his trial, the high priest asked, ‘Are you the Christ, the Son of the Blessed?’ Jesus said, ‘I am; and you will see the Son of man seated at the right hand of Power.’ (Mark 14:62) The high priest tore his robes. Jesus was condemned for blasphemy because he claimed to be God. He accepted worship, from Thomas and others, which a faithful Jew would never accept if he were only a man.”", "Say: “C. S. Lewis made a famous argument. Jesus said he forgave sins and that he was one with God. ‘A man who was merely a man and said the sort of things Jesus said would not be a great moral teacher. He would either be a lunatic—on the level with the man who says he is a poached egg—or else he would be the Devil of Hell. You must make your choice. Either this man was, and is, the Son of God, or else a madman or something worse.’ This is called the trilemma: Lord, liar or lunatic. Critics say there are other options, like legend. But the Gospels, the Resurrection, and the early Christians’ worship of Jesus as Lord point to the Church’s faith. Thomas’s answer is the right one: ‘My Lord and my God.’”"],
    ask=["Name a claim of Jesus that points to divinity. (Forgiving sins; “Before Abraham was, I am”; “I and the Father are one.”)", "Why was Jesus condemned? (For blasphemy; he claimed to be the Son of God.)", "What is Lewis’s trilemma? (Lord, liar or lunatic.)"],
    practice=["List Jesus’ seven “I am” statements with references.", "Write what each claims about him.", "Mark with a star those that clearly claim divinity.", "Write a paragraph answering: “Was Jesus just a good teacher?”"],
    close="Pray Thomas’s words together: “My Lord and my God!” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Say ‘My Lord and my God’ at the elevation at Mass.”")),

'9-6-1': dict(
  saint="Dominic: a preacher who sold his books to feed the hungry and spent his life teaching the truth.",
  tryit=["Map the themes of the Kingdom parables.", "Write the main point of three.", "Say what the Kingdom asks of you."],
  saintStory="Dominic Guzmán was born around 1170 in Castile, Spain. As a student at Palencia, he studied the Bible and theology. During a famine, he sold his books, even those heavily annotated in his own hand, to feed the poor. “Would you have me study from dead skins when men are dying of hunger?” he said.\nAs a priest, he traveled with his bishop to southern France, where many had embraced the Albigensian heresy, which taught that the material world was evil. He saw that preachers there were wealthy and arrogant, while the heretics lived simply. Dominic decided that the way to reach people was to preach, and to do so in poverty, on foot, with deep learning and example. He founded an order of preachers, the Dominicans, approved in 1216.\nHe believed in prayer and study, and he sent his friars to the new universities. A tradition holds that Mary gave him the Rosary. He died in 1221. His motto: to praise, to bless, to preach.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “If someone asked you to explain the main message of Jesus in a minute, what would you say?” Take a few answers. “We’ll see how Jesus did it.”",
    teach=["Say: “Jesus began his public ministry by saying: ‘The time is fulfilled, and the kingdom of God is at hand; repent, and believe in the gospel.’ (Mark 1:15) The Kingdom of God is the central theme of his preaching. Jesus is a teacher with authority; the crowds were ‘astonished at his teaching, for he taught them as one who had authority, and not as the scribes.’ (Mark 1:22) He taught in synagogues, hillsides and boats, and in parables and short sayings. His message: God is a loving Father who seeks the lost; the Kingdom is among us; we must repent, trust and love.”", "Say: “The Kingdom is both present and future. It is present in Jesus himself, in his words and works: ‘If it is by the finger of God that I cast out demons, then the kingdom of God has come upon you.’ (Luke 11:20) It is growing in history, like a mustard seed. And it will be fulfilled at the end of time, when Christ returns. The Church is the seed and beginning of the Kingdom on earth. We pray in the Our Father, ‘Thy kingdom come.’ The Kingdom is God’s reign over hearts, a reign of justice, peace and joy in the Holy Spirit (Romans 14:17).”", "Say: “Jesus’ teaching includes: love of God and neighbor as the greatest commandment; the Sermon on the Mount, with the Beatitudes; the call to forgive; humility (‘the last shall be first’); radical trust in the Father (‘Do not be anxious’); and the demand to take up the cross. He spoke of a narrow gate and wide gate, and the Last Judgment. He called people to follow him as disciples, and he formed the Twelve. Notice how he taught: with questions, stories and example. He did not just give information; he called for decision. ‘Why do you call me Lord, Lord, and not do what I tell you?’ (Luke 6:46)”"],
    ask=["What was the center of Jesus’ preaching? (The Kingdom of God.)", "Is the Kingdom present or future? (Both.)", "How did Jesus teach? (Parables, questions, stories, example, with authority.)"],
    practice=["Choose three Kingdom parables: the sower, the mustard seed, the pearl of great price.", "Write the main point of each in a sentence.", "Write one thing the Kingdom asks of you now.", "Share with a partner."],
    close="Pray the Our Father slowly, pausing at “Thy kingdom come.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Choose one teaching of Jesus to live this week.”")),

'9-6-2': dict(
  saint="Bartimaeus: the blind beggar who shouted until Jesus stopped.",
  tryit=["Classify the miracles in a chart.", "Write what each sign reveals.", "Respond to a skeptic."],
  saintStory="Bartimaeus, “son of Timaeus,” was a blind beggar who sat by the roadside outside Jericho. When he heard that Jesus of Nazareth was passing, he began to shout, “Jesus, Son of David, have mercy on me!” Many in the crowd told him to be quiet. He cried out all the louder.\nJesus stopped and said, “Call him.” They said to the blind man, “Take heart; rise, he is calling you.” Bartimaeus threw off his cloak, probably his only possession, sprang up and came to Jesus. Jesus asked, “What do you want me to do for you?” He said, “Master, let me receive my sight.” Jesus said, “Go your way; your faith has made you well.” Immediately he received his sight and followed Jesus on the way.\nMark remembers his name, which suggests he became a known member of the early Church. He is a model of persistent prayer and of faith.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “If you were blind and could ask Jesus for one thing, would it be easy to ask? What if the crowd told you to be quiet?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “The Gospels record about thirty-five miracles, and say Jesus did many more (John 21:25). They are of four kinds: healings of sickness and disability, exorcisms, nature miracles, and raising the dead. Jesus’ miracles are called ‘signs’ and ‘mighty works.’ They show that the Kingdom has come. They reveal his identity and compassion. He healed lepers, who were outcasts, and he touched them. Many occurred in response to faith: ‘Your faith has made you well.’ At Nazareth, ‘he did not do many mighty works there, because of their unbelief.’ (Matthew 13:58)”", "Say: “A miracle is an event that goes beyond the natural order and is caused by God, a sign to those who see. A natural explanation isn’t always possible. The Church takes miracles seriously and tests them. David Hume argued that miracles are impossible because the evidence of experience is against them. But this assumes the answer. If God exists, miracles are possible. The question is whether the evidence for a particular miracle is good. For the Gospels, the Resurrection is the central miracle. The Church today investigates healings, such as those at Lourdes, with medical panels. Of thousands of claimed cures, only about seventy are recognized as miraculous.”", "Say: “What is the point of a miracle? They are not mere displays of power. Jesus refused to turn stones into bread or jump from the Temple. They point to who he is and call for faith. They show God’s concern for the whole person, body and soul. In John’s Gospel, the signs lead to a discourse: the feeding of the five thousand leads to the Bread of Life; healing of the blind man leads to ‘I am the light of the world’; the raising of Lazarus leads to ‘I am the resurrection and the life.’ Miracles are also signs of the sacraments: Jesus’ healing touch continues in Anointing, his forgiveness in Penance. The greatest miracle is the conversion of a heart.”"],
    ask=["What are four kinds of miracles? (Healings, exorcisms, nature, raising the dead.)", "Why did Jesus work miracles? (To show the Kingdom and reveal who he is, and from compassion.)", "Why did he not do many at Nazareth? (Unbelief.)"],
    practice=["Choose six miracles, such as Mark 2:1-12, 4:35-41, 5:1-20, 10:46-52, John 6:1-14, 11:1-44.", "Make a chart: Miracle, Type, What it reveals about Jesus, What it asks of us.", "Pick the one that speaks to you most.", "Write a short reply to a friend who says miracles are impossible."],
    close="Pray Bartimaeus’s prayer: “Jesus, Son of David, have mercy on me!” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Say the Jesus Prayer five times a day.”")),

'9-6-3': dict(
  saint="Gregory of Nazianzus: a bishop and poet who said that what Christ did not take on, he did not heal.",
  tryit=["List the reasons the Catechism gives for the Incarnation.", "Match each with a verse.", "Say which matters most to you."],
  saintStory="Gregory was born around 329 in Cappadocia, in what is now Turkey. His father was a bishop. He studied in Athens, where he met Basil, and the two became close friends. Gregory wanted a quiet life of study and prayer, but the Church kept calling him out of it. His father pressured him to be ordained a priest; he fled, then returned. Basil made him bishop of a small village that Gregory hated.\nIn 379, the small group of orthodox Christians in Constantinople, where Arians dominated, asked him to lead them. He gave brilliant sermons in a small chapel, his “Theological Orations,” which defended the Trinity and the divinity of Christ. He was made bishop of Constantinople, but disliked the politics, and resigned during the Council of 381.\nHe retired to his family estate and wrote poetry and letters. He died around 390. He is called “the Theologian.” In one letter he wrote: “That which He has not assumed He has not healed; but that which is united to His Godhead is also saved.”",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “If you were drowning and someone could save you by jumping in, but couldn’t swim, would they save you? What would a rescuer need?” Take answers. “Let’s see what Jesus needed to be for us.”",
    teach=["Say: “Why did the Son of God become man? The Catechism gives four reasons (CCC 457-460). One: to save us by reconciling us with God. The Creed says, ‘For us men and for our salvation he came down from heaven.’ ‘God loved us and sent his Son to be the expiation for our sins.’ (1 John 4:10) Two: that we might know God’s love: ‘God so loved the world that he gave his only Son.’ (John 3:16) Three: to be our model of holiness: ‘Learn from me; for I am gentle and lowly in heart.’ (Matthew 11:29) Four: to make us partakers of the divine nature (2 Peter 1:4). ‘The Son of God became man so that we might become God.’”", "Say: “Gregory’s principle explains why the human nature was needed: if Christ did not assume all of human nature, including a human mind and will, then the parts he did not assume were not redeemed. He took on human flesh, mind and soul, to heal all of it. Only God can save us, and only a man can die. Anselm asked in Cur Deus Homo: why did God become man? Because sin offends God infinitely, and humanity owes a debt that humans can’t pay; only God could; but a human must. The God-man pays the debt out of love.”", "Say: “Notice also that Jesus came for a mission: to proclaim the Kingdom, to call sinners (‘I came not to call the righteous, but sinners’), to give his life as a ransom for many (Mark 10:45), to destroy the works of the devil (1 John 3:8), to give life abundantly (John 10:10), to bear witness to the truth (John 18:37), and to establish the Church. There are different theories of the atonement: some stress ransom (we were captives), some satisfaction (a debt), some victory (over sin, death and Satan), some example. The Church holds them together. Salvation means freedom from sin and death, reconciliation with God, adoption as children and the gift of eternal life.”"],
    ask=["What are four reasons for the Incarnation? (To save us, show God’s love, be our model, share his divine life.)", "What did Gregory teach about Christ’s humanity? (What he did not assume, he did not heal.)", "Which verse says Jesus gave his life as a ransom? (Mark 10:45.)"],
    practice=["Make a four-column chart: reason, verse, what it means, what difference it makes to me.", "Fill in each from the Catechism.", "Choose the reason that speaks most to you and write a short paragraph.", "Share it in a small group."],
    close="Pray: “Lord Jesus, you came down for us and for our salvation. Thank you.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Thank Jesus for one reason in particular.”")),

'9-7-1': dict(
  saint="Dismas: the thief on the cross who asked to be remembered.",
  tryit=["Read the accounts of the Passion side by side.", "List who was responsible.", "Explain what the Church teaches about guilt."],
  saintStory="Luke tells us that two criminals were crucified with Jesus, one on his right and one on his left. One of them mocked him: “Are you not the Christ? Save yourself and us!” The other rebuked him: “Do you not fear God, since you are under the same sentence of condemnation? And we indeed justly; for we are receiving the due reward of our deeds; but this man has done nothing wrong.” Then he said, “Jesus, remember me when you come in your kingly power.”\nJesus said to him, “Truly, I say to you, today you will be with me in Paradise.” This is the only person in the Gospels whom Jesus promises Paradise directly.\nThe name Dismas comes from later tradition. He is the patron saint of prisoners and the dying. His story shows the heart of Good Friday: even at the last minute, a sincere prayer for mercy is heard. At the same time, he did not delay till the last minute by plan; no one knows the hour.",
  script=dict(
    open="Be serious. Say, “Today we deal with the central event of Christianity. We’ll be honest about it. Why did Jesus die? Who killed him?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “Jesus was arrested after the Last Supper, tried before the Sanhedrin, and handed to Pilate, who ordered his crucifixion. The Romans carried out the crucifixion, a brutal and shameful punishment for rebels and slaves. Historians agree that this happened; even Tacitus mentions it. But why? On the human level, some of the religious leaders feared him, Pilate feared unrest, Judas betrayed him, Peter denied him, and the crowd shouted. The Church teaches that Jesus’ death cannot be attributed to the Jewish people as a whole, then or now. Vatican II’s Nostra Aetate says so clearly: ‘What happened in his passion cannot be blamed upon all the Jews then living, without distinction, nor upon the Jews of today.’”", "Say: “On the deeper level, the Catechism says that sinners were the authors and agents of the Passion. ‘We must regard as guilty all those who continue to relapse into their sins.’ (CCC 598) Our sins nailed him to the cross. But Jesus freely gave himself: ‘No one takes my life from me, but I lay it down of my own accord.’ (John 10:18) He said at the Last Supper that his body is given for us. The Father did not will the evil that killed his Son, but he willed that the Son would give himself in love. The cross is a sacrifice: Jesus is both priest and victim. It reveals God’s love: ‘Greater love has no man than this, that a man lay down his life for his friends.’ (John 15:13)”", "Say: “How does his death save us? The Letter to the Hebrews describes Christ as the high priest who offers himself once for all. Isaiah 53 says the suffering servant bears our iniquities. Jesus said at the Last Supper, ‘my blood of the covenant, which is poured out for many for the forgiveness of sins.’ (Matthew 26:28) The Church calls this redemption (buying back), reconciliation (restoring friendship), expiation (taking away sin), and sacrifice. Nobody could fully explain, but we trust. The crucifix is central to Catholic life because it shows God’s love for us. It is not defeat but victory: ‘By his wounds we are healed.’”"],
    ask=["Who is responsible for Jesus’ death? (All sinners; not the Jewish people as a whole.)", "What did Jesus say of his life? (No one takes it; I lay it down of my own accord.)", "What does the Catechism say sinners did? (They were the authors and agents of the Passion.)"],
    practice=["Read Mark 15:21-39 and Luke 23:33-49 side by side.", "Make a chart: who was there, what each did, what they said.", "Mark where there are signs of faith or mercy.", "Write a reflection: “At the cross, I stand with…”"],
    close="Pray Dismas’s prayer: “Jesus, remember me when you come into your kingdom.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray before a crucifix tonight.”")),

'9-7-2': dict(
  saint="Joseph of Arimathea: a secret disciple who found the courage to ask Pilate for the body.",
  tryit=["Weigh the explanations for the empty tomb.", "Test each against the evidence.", "State the Church’s claim."],
  saintStory="Joseph of Arimathea was a rich man and a member of the council, the Sanhedrin, who had not consented to the plan to condemn Jesus. John says he was a disciple, but secretly, for fear of the Jews. After Jesus died, Joseph went boldly to Pilate and asked for the body. Pilate checked that Jesus was already dead and gave permission.\nWith Nicodemus, who brought about seventy-five pounds of myrrh and aloes, he took the body down, wrapped it in linen cloths with spices, according to Jewish burial custom, and laid it in his own new tomb, cut in rock, where no one had yet been laid. They rolled a large stone across the entrance. It was late on Friday, just before the Sabbath began.\nHis courage came at the moment when the Twelve had fled. By giving Jesus a proper burial, he became an unexpected witness: the tomb was known. Everyone could later check where the body had been placed.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say, “Imagine the day after Good Friday. Jesus is dead. His followers are scattered. What do you think happens next, by normal expectations? Would the movement continue?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Read Matthew 28:1-10 and 1 Corinthians 15:3-8. Say: “The Resurrection is the central event of the Christian faith. The earliest creed, in Paul, written around AD 55 but handed on from earlier, says that Jesus died, was buried, was raised on the third day, and appeared to Peter, the Twelve, more than 500 at once, James and Paul. The Gospels say the tomb was found empty by women on Sunday morning. The women were the first witnesses, which an invented story in that culture would probably not use, since women’s testimony was not accepted as legal evidence.”", "Say: “What explanations are there? The disciples stole the body: but they would not die for something they knew was a lie. The authorities moved it: they would have produced the body to stop the movement. The women went to the wrong tomb: Joseph’s tomb was well known. Jesus did not really die (swoon theory): the Romans were experts, and he was pierced with a spear. The apostles hallucinated: hallucinations are individual, not shared by groups, and do not explain the empty tomb. A legend developed: the creed in 1 Corinthians 15 comes within a few years. The simplest explanation is what the apostles said: he rose.”", "Say: “The Church teaches that the Resurrection is a real event in history, and also transcends history. Jesus was not resuscitated, like Lazarus, but rose with a glorified body, unlimited by space and death. He could appear in locked rooms, yet showed his wounds and ate fish. The resurrection is the foundation: ‘If Christ has not been raised, then our preaching is in vain.’ It confirms his divinity, shows God accepted his sacrifice, defeats death, and is the source of our own resurrection and justification (Romans 4:25). We celebrate it every Sunday and especially at Easter. Faith in it is a gift, but the evidence is substantial.”"],
    ask=["What are some alternative explanations for the empty tomb? (Stolen body, wrong tomb, swoon, hallucination, legend.)", "Why are women as first witnesses significant? (An invented story would not use them in that culture.)", "How is Jesus’ resurrection different from Lazarus’s? (He rose to a glorified life; Lazarus returned to ordinary life and died again.)"],
    practice=["Make a table: Explanation, Evidence for, Problems with it.", "Include six explanations.", "Rate each: very plausible, plausible, unlikely.", "Write a paragraph on which explanation best fits all the facts."],
    close="Pray: “Christ is risen! He is risen indeed!” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Read 1 Corinthians 15:3-8 aloud.”")),

'9-7-3': dict(
  saint="Vincent Ferrer: a preacher who warned of the Last Judgment and brought people to repentance.",
  tryit=["Lay out the end-times events in order.", "Mark what Scripture says and what it does not.", "Write what living ready looks like."],
  saintStory="Vincent Ferrer was born in Valencia, Spain, around 1350. He became a Dominican, and was a brilliant professor. In 1398, during a serious illness in Avignon, he said that Jesus, with Dominic and Francis, appeared to him and told him to go out and preach repentance. He recovered and spent the next twenty years traveling on foot and by donkey throughout Spain, France, Italy and the Low Countries, preaching to huge crowds. He spoke in Catalan, and yet people of other languages claimed to understand him.\nHis theme: “Fear God and give him glory, for the hour of his judgment has come.” His sermons called people to repent, to forgive their enemies and to prepare for death. Thousands of sinners were reconciled. He also worked to end the Western Schism, when there were rival claimants to the papacy, and he supported a council that ended it.\nHe died in 1419 in Vannes. He is patron of builders and of Valencia. He taught that to prepare for the Judgment means to love now.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “What do you hope will be said about you at the end of your life? What would you want to have done?” Take a few answers. “That’s a question about the Last Day.”",
    teach=["Read Acts 1:6-11. Say: “Forty days after the Resurrection, Jesus ascended into heaven. This means that Jesus, in his glorified human body, returned to the Father, to be our advocate. He sits at the right hand of the Father, a sign of authority. He is not absent: ‘I am with you always.’ (Matthew 28:20) He intercedes for us (Hebrews 7:25), prepares a place for us (John 14:2), and sends the Spirit. The angels said, ‘This Jesus… will come in the same way as you saw him go.’”", "Say: “The Second Coming of Christ is called the Parousia, Greek for ‘presence’ or ‘arrival.’ The Creed says, ‘He will come again in glory to judge the living and the dead, and his kingdom will have no end.’ When? ‘Of that day or that hour no one knows, not even the angels in heaven, nor the Son, but only the Father.’ (Mark 13:32) The Church warns against predicting dates. Before the end, the Church must go through a final trial (CCC 675), but we don’t know the details. The Church prays ‘Marana tha,’ ‘Come, Lord.’ Every Mass says it: ‘We proclaim your death, O Lord, and profess your Resurrection until you come again.’”", "Say: “The Church teaches that there are two judgments. The particular judgment: at death, each soul is judged and goes to heaven, purgatory or hell. The general or Last Judgment: at the end of time, Christ will come, the dead will be raised, and the whole truth of each life will be revealed. Matthew 25:31-46: we will be judged on love for the least of his brothers. This is not meant to frighten but to motivate. The resurrection of the body: at the end, our bodies will be raised, glorified. And there will be new heavens and a new earth. Vigilance means living now in a way that welcomes Christ. Jesus said, ‘Watch therefore, for you know neither the day nor the hour.’”"],
    ask=["What does the Ascension mean? (Jesus returned to the Father in his glorified humanity.)", "What is the Parousia? (Christ’s Second Coming.)", "What are the two judgments? (Particular at death; general at the end.)"],
    practice=["Make a timeline: Ascension, the Church’s age, the final trial, the Parousia, the resurrection of the dead, the Last Judgment, new heavens and earth.", "Mark the parts the Bible clearly states and those it does not.", "Read Matthew 25:31-46 and list the questions the Judge asks.", "Write three habits of living ready."],
    close="Pray: “Come, Lord Jesus.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Do one of the works of mercy from Matthew 25 this week.”")),

'9-8-1': dict(
  saint="Andrew Kim Taegon: Korea’s first native priest, who died at twenty-five for the faith his people had started on their own.",
  tryit=["Trace the steps of discipleship in Mark.", "Mark the costs.", "Write your own “yes.”"],
  saintStory="Korea’s Church began without missionaries. In 1784, a young scholar, Yi Seung-hun, was baptized in Beijing and returned to Korea, where he started to baptize others. The small community of lay people grew for decades before any priest arrived. They suffered severe persecution.\nAndrew Kim Taegon was born in 1821 into a Christian family; his great-grandfather and father were both martyred. At fifteen, he was sent to Macau to train for the priesthood, and was ordained in Shanghai in 1845, the first Korean priest. He returned secretly to Korea by sea, and ministered to Christians. In 1846, he was arrested while trying to bring in more missionaries.\nIn prison he wrote letters encouraging the faithful. He was tortured and beheaded on September 16, 1846, at twenty-five. He is one of 103 Korean martyrs canonized in 1984. A Korean Church that began with lay people has grown into one of the largest in Asia.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “What is something you committed to that was harder than you expected, like a team, an instrument or a promise? What kept you going?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “The Gospel of Mark has a clear structure about discipleship. Jesus calls: ‘Follow me, and I will make you fishers of men.’ (Mark 1:17) The first disciples left their nets at once. He appoints the Twelve ‘to be with him, and to be sent out.’ He teaches and shows them signs. At Caesarea Philippi, Peter says, ‘You are the Christ.’ Immediately Jesus teaches them that the Son of Man must suffer, and Peter rebukes him. Jesus says, ‘Get behind me, Satan.’ Then he gives the cost: ‘If any man would come after me, let him deny himself and take up his cross and follow me.’ (Mark 8:34)”", "Say: “Discipleship is a relationship before it’s a rule. It begins with a call, a response, and a life lived with Jesus. The disciples were slow to understand, they argued about who was greatest, they fell asleep in Gethsemane, they fled, and Peter denied him. Yet Jesus did not give up on them. After the Resurrection, he restored Peter. Discipleship is about conversion, which is lifelong. Faith is an act of the whole person: the mind accepts, the will chooses, the heart loves, and it shows in life. James writes, ‘Faith by itself, if it has no works, is dead.’”", "Say: “What does it cost? Jesus is clear: the rich young man went away sad because he had many possessions. ‘Whoever loves father or mother more than me is not worthy of me.’ We are asked to put Jesus first, to take up our cross daily, to forgive, to serve. It also gives much: ‘Everyone who has left houses or brothers or sisters… for my name’s sake, will receive a hundredfold and inherit eternal life.’ (Matthew 19:29) Discipleship is lived in the Church, with others. The vocation of the baptized is to be missionary disciples. Each of us can ask: What is Jesus calling me to now?”"],
    ask=["What did Jesus say is the cost of discipleship? (Deny yourself, take up your cross and follow him.)", "How did the disciples respond to Jesus’ suffering? (They struggled, fled, and later returned.)", "What does the Church ask of disciples? (Faith lived out in love.)"],
    practice=["Read Mark 1:16-20, 8:27-38, 10:17-31.", "Make a chart: Call, Response, Cost, Reward.", "Write your own “yes” to Jesus: one specific thing you will do this month to follow him more closely.", "Share it with a partner for accountability."],
    close="Pray the prayer of discipleship: “Lord, I want to follow you. Show me the way.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Take one small step toward Jesus each day.”")),

'9-8-2': dict(
  saint="Matt Talbot: a Dublin laborer who found grace in the freedom from alcohol.",
  tryit=["Define grace in your own words.", "Mark what God does and what we do.", "Write the story of a change."],
  saintStory="Matt Talbot was born in 1856 in Dublin, one of twelve children in a poor family. He left school early, and started work at twelve in a wine merchant’s store. By then he had already started drinking. He drank heavily for sixteen years, spending his wages and even pawning his boots and clothes. His days were a blur.\nIn 1884, after a weekend with no money, he stood outside a pub waiting for friends to invite him in. No one did. He went home, and told his mother he would take “the pledge.” He took it for three months, and then for life. The first months were hard; he went to Mass every morning, prayed constantly and fell on his knees to ask for help when the temptation came.\nHe worked as a laborer for the rest of his life, gave much of his money to the poor, and lived simply, praying and fasting. He died on his way to Mass in 1925. Many who struggle with addiction pray to him.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Can a person lift themselves out of a deep pit by pulling on their own hair? What do they need?” Take answers. “That’s a question about grace.”",
    teach=["Say: “Grace is the free and undeserved help that God gives us to respond to his call, to become his children, and to share in his life. The Catechism defines it as ‘favor, the free and undeserved help that God gives us.’ (CCC 1996) We cannot earn it. Paul wrote, ‘For by grace you have been saved through faith; and this is not your own doing, it is the gift of God.’ (Ephesians 2:8) Sanctifying grace is a permanent disposition that makes us holy and friends of God. Actual grace is a particular help in a moment. Sacramental grace is given by each sacrament.”", "Say: “Justification is God’s work of making us righteous. It is the beginning of our conversion. In Baptism, God forgives our sins and makes us his children. The Council of Trent taught that justification is not only the forgiveness of sins but the sanctification and renewal of the interior man. It comes from God’s grace, received through faith and the sacraments, and it makes us truly just. We cooperate: grace doesn’t override freedom. We can say yes or no. ‘God, who created you without you, will not save you without you,’ said Augustine.”", "Say: “Catholics and Protestants have disagreed about justification. Luther taught that we are justified by faith alone and that righteousness is credited to us from outside. The Catholic Church teaches that faith is the beginning, and that love and good works are fruits of grace. Paul wrote that ‘a man is justified by faith apart from works of law’ (Romans 3:28), meaning works done without Christ, and James wrote that ‘a man is justified by works and not by faith alone’ (James 2:24). We can’t merit the first grace. After that, God’s grace enables us to do works that are meritorious because they are God’s gift. The 1999 Catholic-Lutheran Joint Declaration said we share a basic consensus: ‘By grace alone, in faith in Christ’s saving work… we are accepted by God.’”"],
    ask=["What is grace? (God’s free and undeserved help.)", "Can we earn the first grace? (No; it is a gift.)", "Does grace remove freedom? (No; we cooperate.)"],
    practice=["Draw two columns: “What God does” and “What I do.”", "Fill in, from Ephesians 2:8-10, James 2:14-26 and the lesson.", "Write a short true or imagined story of a change (like Matt Talbot’s), marking where grace came and where a choice was made.", "Share with a partner."],
    close="Pray: “By your grace, Lord, I am what I am.” (1 Corinthians 15:10.) Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Thank God for a grace you have received.”")),

'9-8-3': dict(
  saint="Mary: the mother who pondered everything in her heart.",
  tryit=["Pray a Gospel scene using imagination.", "Write what you saw, heard and said.", "End with a conversation with Jesus."],
  saintStory="Luke twice says that Mary kept things and pondered them in her heart. After the shepherds came to the stable and told what the angels had said, “Mary kept all these things, pondering them in her heart.” After finding the twelve-year-old Jesus in the Temple, and hearing him say that he must be in his Father’s house, “his mother kept all these things in her heart.”\nMary did not always understand. At the Annunciation she asked, “How can this be?” At the Temple, “they did not understand the saying which he spoke to them.” But she did not push the mystery away. She turned it over and held it. Over decades she watched, remembered, and prayed with what she saw. At Cana, she said to the servants, “Do whatever he tells you.” At the cross she stood. In the upper room she prayed with the apostles.\nThe Church sees Mary as the first disciple and the model of contemplation. The Rosary invites us to look at Christ with her eyes.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “When you read a story, can you see it in your mind? Close your eyes and picture your bedroom. Now imagine you’re in a story. We’re going to use imagination to pray.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Gospels are not just stories from the past; they are living words. The Church has many ways to pray with Scripture. Lectio divina, ‘sacred reading,’ has four steps: lectio (read the passage slowly), meditatio (reflect on what it says), oratio (respond to God in prayer) and contemplatio (rest in God’s presence). Ignatius of Loyola taught another way: Gospel contemplation, imagining yourself in a scene. See the people, hear the words, feel what happens. Ask what Jesus says to you. This is not make-believe; it engages our mind and heart with God’s word.”", "Say: “Here’s how to do it. First, pray for the Spirit. Second, read the passage slowly, and read it a second time. Third, imagine the scene: What does the place look like? Who is there? What are they feeling? Put yourself in the scene, perhaps as a bystander or as one of the characters. Fourth, notice what Jesus says and does. How does he look at you? Fifth, speak to him as to a friend: tell him what you feel, ask for what you need, thank him. Sixth, close with an Our Father, and note what stood out. You may find yourself surprised. Write it in a journal.”", "Say: “The Gospels also help us pray the Rosary, which Pope John Paul II called ‘a compendium of the Gospel.’ In the Rosary, we meditate on twenty mysteries: Joyful (Annunciation, Visitation, Nativity, Presentation, Finding in the Temple), Luminous (Baptism, Cana, Proclamation of the Kingdom, Transfiguration, Eucharist), Sorrowful (Agony, Scourging, Crowning with Thorns, Carrying the Cross, Crucifixion) and Glorious (Resurrection, Ascension, Descent of the Spirit, Assumption, Coronation). While the lips say the Hail Mary, the mind contemplates the scene with Mary. Regular prayer with Scripture is one of the best ways to get to know Jesus. He is not just a figure in a book but is present and speaks.”"],
    ask=["What are the four steps of lectio divina? (Read, meditate, pray, contemplate.)", "How do you pray with imagination? (Picture the scene, place yourself in it, speak with Jesus.)", "What are the four sets of Rosary mysteries? (Joyful, Luminous, Sorrowful, Glorious.)"],
    practice=["Choose a short Gospel passage such as Mark 10:46-52 or Luke 19:1-10.", "Pray it using the six steps in silence for ten minutes.", "Write what you saw, what Jesus said, and what you answered.", "Share a small part with a partner if you wish."],
    close="Pray the Hail Mary with the mystery of the Visitation in mind. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Spend ten minutes praying with a Gospel scene.”")),

'9-9-1': dict(
  saint="Peter Damian: a reformer who told popes the truth about the Church’s sin.",
  tryit=["Read the Church’s steps of reform.", "Mark which protect children.", "Write what a safe community needs."],
  saintStory="Peter Damian was born in Ravenna, Italy, around 1007, the youngest of a large poor family. His brother, a priest, paid for his schooling. He became a brilliant teacher, then entered a hermitage at Fonte Avellana, where he lived a life of prayer and severe penance. In 1043 he became the prior.\nThe Church of his day was in need of reform: some bishops bought their positions, some priests were not celibate, and the clergy were poorly trained. Peter wrote letters and treatises demanding reform. In about 1049 he wrote the Book of Gomorrah, addressed to Pope Leo IX, which denounced sexual abuse and immorality among the clergy, including the abuse of those they were supposed to serve. He asked the pope to act. Leo IX received it with some sympathy but did not take all the steps Peter asked.\nPeter was made a cardinal in 1057, and served as a legate. He died in 1072. He is a Doctor of the Church. He shows that the Church has always needed reform, and that saints have told the truth about it.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say carefully and seriously, “This lesson is about something painful. We’ll speak honestly, with respect for survivors. If you have been hurt by anyone, you are never alone, and you can talk to a trusted adult at any time.” Pause. “Is it hard for people to believe that someone who is supposed to be good can do evil? Why?”",
    teach=["Say: “The Church is holy because Christ is holy, but her members are sinners, including priests and bishops. Jesus chose Judas, and Judas betrayed him. Throughout history, the Church has had scandals and has needed reform. The sexual abuse of minors by clergy is among the worst. Abuse is a grave sin and a crime. It harms victims profoundly, damaging trust, mental health and faith. It betrays the sacred trust that priests have. And the Church’s response was, in many places, inadequate: bishops sometimes moved abusers from parish to parish, hid the cases, and failed to listen to victims.”", "Say: “The scale became public in the United States in 2002, when the Boston Globe reported on cases. The John Jay study (2004), commissioned by the bishops, found that about 4 percent of priests serving between 1950 and 2002 were accused, with over 10,000 victims reported, most in the 1960s-1980s. Similar crises came out in Ireland, Australia, Germany, Chile and elsewhere. A Pennsylvania grand jury report in 2018 documented abuse by over 300 priests. The Church has acknowledged these failures. Pope Benedict XVI met with survivors; Pope Francis called the sin and crime ‘a monstrosity’ and acknowledged cover-ups. Victims deserve justice and healing.”", "Say: “What reforms have happened? In 2002, the U.S. bishops adopted the Charter for the Protection of Children and Young People, known as the Dallas Charter: zero tolerance for abusers; every diocese must have a victim assistance coordinator and review board; background checks and safe environment training for all clergy, employees and volunteers who work with children; and reporting abuse allegations to civil authorities. In 2019, Pope Francis issued Vos estis lux mundi, requiring clergy to report abuse and creating procedures to investigate bishops. Many dioceses have compensation and counseling programs. The rate of new abuse has fallen sharply. But the Church must remain vigilant. Reform is continuous. In addition, abuse happens in families, schools and sports too, and is a wider problem. What can we do? Know the safe-environment rules; if anyone, in any setting, makes you uncomfortable, tell a trusted adult. Support survivors and believe them. Report. Pray. And hold the Church accountable.”"],
    ask=["Is abuse a sin or a crime? (Both.)", "What was the Dallas Charter? (A 2002 set of U.S. bishops’ policies: zero tolerance, review boards, background checks, reporting.)", "What should a young person do if made uncomfortable? (Tell a trusted adult.)"],
    practice=["Work in small groups to write a list of what a safe parish needs: background checks, two adults present, clear reporting, listening to victims, training, transparency.", "Write a contact list of trusted adults you could go to.", "Write a prayer for survivors.", "Discuss: How can the Church rebuild trust?"],
    close="Pray for survivors: “Lord, heal those who have been wounded, and purify your Church.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “If you have a concern, speak to a trusted adult.”")),

'9-9-2': dict(
  saint="Hildegard of Bingen: an abbess whose counsel was sought by popes and emperors.",
  tryit=["List the Church’s reasons for an all-male priesthood.", "List the roles women have had in the Church.", "Write your own summary."],
  saintStory="Hildegard was born in 1098 in the Rhineland, the tenth child of a noble family, and was given to the Church as a tithe at about eight. She grew up in the care of an anchoress, Jutta, and became a Benedictine nun. She experienced visions from childhood, but kept them secret until she was in her forties, when she began to write them down in Scivias.\nShe became abbess, founded her own convent at Rupertsberg, and wrote on theology, medicine, natural history, and wrote and composed music for the convent. She corresponded with popes, kings and bishops, including Bernard of Clairvaux, and gave advice (sometimes sharp). She preached in public, which was highly unusual for a woman, traveling on four preaching tours in Germany.\nHildegard died in 1179. In 2012 Pope Benedict XVI named her a Doctor of the Church. She shows that women have held great influence in the Church, although the priesthood is not open to them.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “What are some roles in the Church? Priests, deacons, nuns, catechists, readers. Which can women do? Which can’t they?” List them. Say, “This is a question many people ask, including Catholics. We will give the Church’s teaching and the reasons honestly.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Catholic Church teaches that priestly ordination is reserved to men. In 1994, Pope John Paul II wrote in Ordinatio Sacerdotalis, ‘The Church has no authority whatsoever to confer priestly ordination on women and this judgment is to be definitively held by all the Church’s faithful.’ The Congregation for the Doctrine of the Faith said in 1995 that this teaching belongs to the deposit of faith. The reasons are not that women are less than men; the Church insists on the equal dignity of men and women, both made in God’s image.”", "Say: “Reasons given. One: Jesus’ example. He chose twelve men as apostles, though he was countercultural in other ways with women: he taught them, he had women disciples, and women were first at the empty tomb. He could have chosen women, but did not. Two: the apostles’ practice. They chose men as successors, including Matthias, Paul, Timothy and Titus. Three: constant Tradition for 2,000 years, in the East and West. Four: the priest acts in persona Christi capitis, in the person of Christ, the Bridegroom of the Church; the sacramental sign reflects the relationship of Christ and the Church, as bridegroom and bride. Five: the Church sees herself bound by Christ’s choice, as she is with the matter of the Eucharist (bread and wine).”", "Say: “Some Catholics and many others disagree, saying that the exclusion is cultural. We should be respectful and understand the reasons. Also, the Church teaches that priesthood is not a matter of status or power but of service. Women in the Church have always had great roles. Mary, the Mother of God, is the highest creature, and she was not a priest. There are women Doctors of the Church (Teresa, Catherine, Thérèse, Hildegard), foundresses of orders, missionaries, theologians, teachers, administrators, chancellors, heads of religious orders and Catholic hospitals. Women make up the majority of lay ministers. Pope Francis has appointed women to senior Vatican positions. Regarding women deacons, the Church has studied the question; in the early Church there were deaconesses, such as Phoebe, but their role is debated, and a commission studied it with no consensus. We pray for the Church to discern the Spirit’s will.”"],
    ask=["What did John Paul II teach in Ordinatio Sacerdotalis? (The Church has no authority to ordain women.)", "Name two reasons given. (Jesus’ choice of twelve men; constant Tradition; the priest acts in the person of Christ.)", "Name some roles of women in the Church. (Doctors, foundresses, theologians, lay ministers, Vatican officials.)"],
    practice=["Make two columns: “The Church’s reasons” and “Objections and responses.”", "Fill in honestly, using the lesson and Ordinatio Sacerdotalis.", "Add a list of women saints and their influence.", "Write a paragraph: how would I explain this teaching respectfully to a friend who disagrees?"],
    close="Pray: “Lord, help us honor the gifts of every member of your Church.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Read about a woman Doctor of the Church.”")),

'9-9-3': dict(
  saint="Solanus Casey: a Capuchin porter whose simple welcome comforted thousands.",
  tryit=["Read Matthew 19:12 and 1 Corinthians 7:32-35.", "Write the reasons for celibacy.", "Write the objections and replies."],
  saintStory="Bernard Casey was born in 1870 in Wisconsin, one of sixteen children of Irish immigrants. He worked as a lumberjack, hospital orderly and trolley car operator. After witnessing a violent incident, he felt the call to be a priest. He joined the Capuchin Franciscans, taking the name Solanus.\nHis seminary studies were hard. He struggled in classes taught in German and Latin, and when he was ordained in 1904, he was allowed to be a “simplex priest,” which meant he could celebrate Mass but not preach doctrinal homilies or hear confessions. He was assigned as a porter, the monastery doorkeeper, in New York, and then in Detroit. For decades he greeted visitors, listened to people with problems, and prayed with them. Many reported healings and graces. During the Depression, he helped start a soup kitchen that fed thousands daily; it is still running.\nHe died in 1957. He was beatified in 2017. A priest who never preached a sermon became known for the consolation he gave.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Why do you think some people choose not to marry? What might they gain? What might they give up?” Take answers carefully and respectfully.",
    teach=["Say: “In the Latin (Roman) Catholic Church, priests promise celibacy: they don’t marry. In Eastern Catholic Churches, married men may be ordained priests, but they cannot marry after ordination; bishops are chosen from the unmarried. In the Latin Church, married men may become permanent deacons. In some special cases, former Anglican or Lutheran ministers who are married and become Catholic can be ordained priests. So celibacy is a discipline, not a doctrine; the Church could change it, though she has not.”", "Say: “The reasons come from Scripture and tradition. Jesus was celibate, and he said, ‘There are eunuchs who have made themselves eunuchs for the sake of the kingdom of heaven. He who is able to receive this, let him receive it.’ (Matthew 19:12) Paul, also celibate, wrote that the unmarried man is anxious about the affairs of the Lord, how to please the Lord, whereas the married man is anxious about worldly affairs, how to please his wife (1 Corinthians 7:32-34). Celibacy is a sign of the Kingdom, where ‘they neither marry nor are given in marriage.’ (Matthew 22:30) It lets the priest give his whole heart to God and his people, to be a spiritual father to many. It images Christ, who was celibate and wholly given to the Church.”", "Say: “Historically, many early priests were married, but from early centuries the Church encouraged or required continence. The First Lateran Council in 1123 and the Second in 1139 made marriage invalid for priests in the West. The discipline was strengthened at Trent. Critics argue that celibacy discourages vocations and contributes to scandals; the Church answers that celibacy is not the cause of abuse (most abusers in society are not celibate), and the shortage in vocations is also a matter of faith. Celibacy is a gift that must be freely embraced, with grace, prayer, friendships and community. Many priests live it joyfully. It does not mean a priest is not loving. Some priests find it hard, and we should pray for them. The Church also honors married love as holy: marriage is a sacrament.”"],
    ask=["Is celibacy a doctrine? (No; it’s a discipline that could change.)", "Can married men be priests? (In Eastern Churches, yes; in the Latin Church, only in special cases like former Anglican ministers; permanent deacons can be married.)", "What are the reasons for celibacy? (Imitating Christ, undivided service, sign of the Kingdom.)"],
    practice=["Make a chart: Reasons for celibacy / Questions and objections / Responses.", "Fill in honestly.", "Add a row: how the Church honors both marriage and celibacy.", "Write a prayer for your parish priest."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, bless our priests and give them joy.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Thank a priest, and ask him what he loves about his vocation.”")),

'9-10-1': dict(
  saint="John Henry Newman: an Oxford scholar who studied history and became Catholic.",
  tryit=["Test Newman’s seven notes on a doctrine.", "Trace one doctrine through the centuries.", "Explain how it grew."],
  saintStory="John Henry Newman was born in London in 1801. He became an Anglican priest and a fellow at Oxford, and a leader of the Oxford Movement, which sought to recover the Church of England’s Catholic heritage. His sermons at St. Mary’s drew crowds of students.\nAs he studied the early Church to defend the Anglican position, he found that the evidence kept pointing to Rome. He wrote, “To be deep in history is to cease to be Protestant.” He puzzled over how the Church today could be the same as the Church of the apostles when so much seemed different. His answer was An Essay on the Development of Christian Doctrine, which he wrote in 1845 and left unfinished when he was received into the Catholic Church on October 9, 1845. It cost him friends and position.\nHe became a priest, founded the Oratory in Birmingham, and was made a cardinal in 1879. He died in 1890 and was canonized in 2019. His motto: Cor ad cor loquitur, “Heart speaks to heart.”",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Is an oak tree the same as the acorn it grew from? Is the adult the same as the baby photo? What changes and what stays?” Take answers. “Now ask the same about the Church’s teaching.”",
    teach=["Say: “Critics of the Church say her teaching has changed: ‘The Church today teaches things that weren’t taught in the first centuries.’ The Church answers that revelation was complete with Christ and the apostles, but our understanding of it grows. Vatican II said: ‘There is a growth in the understanding of the realities and the words which have been handed down… through the contemplation and study made by believers… through a penetrating understanding of the spiritual realities which they experience… through the preaching of those who have received the sure gift of truth.’ (DV 8)”", "Say: “Vincent of Lérins compared this to a human body: it grows and changes, but remains the same person. ‘The religion of souls should imitate the manner of bodies, which, though in the course of years they develop and disclose their members, yet remain the same.’ Newman gave tests for genuine development as opposed to corruption: preservation of type (it’s the same species), continuity of principles, power of assimilation, logical sequence, anticipation of its future, conservative action upon its past, and chronic vigor. A true development clarifies; a corruption contradicts.”", "Say: “Examples of development. The Trinity: the New Testament teaches Father, Son and Spirit; the Church worked out the language of ‘one substance, three Persons’ to defend it. The divinity of Christ: held from the start, defined at Nicaea. The canon of Scripture. The Eucharist: from ‘This is my body’ to ‘transubstantiation.’ The Immaculate Conception and the Assumption: implicit in Mary as ‘full of grace’ and the Fathers; defined in 1854 and 1950. The teaching on slavery: always seen as a sin in principle (Paul’s letter to Philemon), but the Church’s moral witness became clearer over time. Religious freedom: Vatican II explained that coercion in matters of faith is contrary to dignity, drawing out something already in the tradition. The Church’s doctrine does not change in meaning but is understood more deeply. As Newman wrote, ‘In a higher world it is otherwise, but here below to live is to change, and to be perfect is to have changed often.’”"],
    ask=["Does doctrine change? (Its meaning does not change; our understanding grows.)", "What is the difference between development and corruption? (A development keeps the same principles and clarifies; a corruption contradicts.)", "Give an example of development. (The Trinity, the canon, the Assumption.)"],
    practice=["Pick one doctrine (Trinity, Real Presence, Mary’s Assumption, or religious freedom).", "Trace it in a timeline: Scripture, early Fathers, councils, modern definitions.", "Test it against two of Newman’s notes.", "Write a paragraph: “How is this the same faith, grown?”"],
    close="Pray Newman’s prayer: “Lead, kindly light.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Read ‘Lead, Kindly Light.’”")),

'9-10-2': dict(
  saint="Maximus the Confessor: a monk who held to Christ’s two wills though they cut out his tongue.",
  tryit=["Sort teachings by level on the hierarchy.", "Say which are central.", "Explain why order matters."],
  saintStory="Maximus was born around 580 in Constantinople, and served at the imperial court before becoming a monk. He became a great theologian. In the seventh century, a controversy broke out: Monothelitism taught that Christ had only one will, a divine will. The emperor and the patriarch of Constantinople supported it as a compromise to unite the Church. Maximus argued that if Christ lacked a human will, then he had not fully taken on human nature, and our salvation would be incomplete: Christ freely chose to obey the Father, in Gethsemane: “Not my will, but yours.”\nMaximus was arrested in 653 and taken to Constantinople. He refused to compromise. In 662, after a trial, his tongue and right hand were cut off, so that he could neither speak nor write against the imperial position. He was exiled to the Caucasus, where he died on August 13, 662. In 681, the Third Council of Constantinople vindicated his teaching: Christ has two wills.\nHe is called the Confessor, one who suffers for the faith without dying.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “If a ship were leaking, would you first bail water or check the paint? In what order should we take care of things?” Take answers. “The Church’s teaching has an order too.”",
    teach=["Say: “A dogma is a truth contained in divine revelation that the Church has defined and proposed as necessary to believe. Dogmas must be believed by all with divine and Catholic faith. They’re not arbitrary rules but signposts that light up and protect the way. The Creed is a summary of dogmas. Dogmas include the Trinity, the Incarnation, the Resurrection, the Real Presence, and the Marian dogmas. Rejecting a dogma knowingly is heresy; the sin of leaving the faith entirely is apostasy; of leaving the Church’s unity is schism. These involve a serious break.”", "Say: “Vatican II taught that there is a ‘hierarchy’ of truths, because their connection with the foundation of the Christian faith is different. (Unitatis Redintegratio 11) It does not mean some truths can be ignored. All truths revealed by God must be believed. But some are more central. At the top: the Trinity, the Incarnation and Redemption in Christ, the Paschal Mystery. Closer to the foundation than a particular devotion or discipline. Pope Francis in 2013: ‘The Church’s ministers must be merciful… the proclamation of the saving love of God comes before moral and religious imperatives.’ A good teacher presents the core first.”", "Say: “The Church’s teaching has levels. Dogmas of faith (divinely revealed and defined). Definitive doctrines (necessarily connected with revelation, such as the exclusion of women from priesthood or the invalidity of Anglican orders). Authoritative teaching that requires religious assent. Prudential judgments and disciplines (fasting rules, the language of the liturgy, celibacy for the clergy). Customs and devotions (processions, particular prayers). Disciplines can change; dogma cannot. Knowing the difference helps avoid two errors: treating all rules as equally absolute, or treating everything as negotiable. The Creed is central: ‘In essentials, unity; in doubtful matters, liberty; in all things, charity.’”"],
    ask=["What is a dogma? (A truth in revelation defined by the Church as necessary to believe.)", "What does the hierarchy of truths mean? (Some truths are more central than others, though all must be believed.)", "Can disciplines change? (Yes; dogma cannot.)"],
    practice=["Prepare a list: the Trinity, meatless Fridays, the Real Presence, Latin Mass, the Assumption, celibacy for priests, the Creed, Marian devotion, the Rosary.", "Sort into five levels.", "Mark the three most central.", "Write why the order matters for explaining the faith."],
    close="Pray the Apostles’ Creed. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pick one line from the Creed and learn its meaning.”")),

'9-10-3': dict(
  saint="Juan Diego: a layman who carried the Gospel in his cloak.",
  tryit=["List ways the laity hand on the faith.", "Mark the ones you can do now.", "Write a plan."],
  saintStory="Juan Diego Cuauhtlatoatzin was born in 1474 near Mexico City, an indigenous Aztec. He and his wife were among the first baptized by the Franciscans in about 1524. In December 1531, as he walked to Mass on Tepeyac Hill, he saw a young woman who spoke to him in his own language, Nahuatl. She said she was the Virgin Mary, and asked him to tell the bishop to build a church there.\nThe bishop, Juan de Zumárraga, did not believe him and asked for a sign. Juan Diego went back, and Mary told him to gather roses blooming out of season on the hill. He carried them in his tilma, a cloak of cactus fiber, to the bishop. When he opened it, the roses fell out, and on the cloth was an image of the Virgin. The tilma is still preserved at the Basilica of Our Lady of Guadalupe, and within a few years millions of Mexicans became Catholic.\nJuan Diego lived the rest of his life near the chapel. He was canonized in 2002. Though an ordinary layman, he carried the message.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Who passed on your faith to you? Think of one person. Were they a priest?” Take answers. “Most of us learned from a parent, grandparent or teacher.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Church teaches that the faith is handed on in many ways. The Magisterium teaches authoritatively, but the whole Church is involved. Dei Verbum says that Tradition ‘makes progress in the Church… through the contemplation and study made by believers, who treasure these things in their hearts; it comes from the intimate understanding of spiritual things they experience; and it comes from the preaching of those who have received the sure gift of truth.’ The faithful, the laity, have a role. Parents are the first teachers of their children’s faith.”", "Say: “The sensus fidei, the sense of the faith, is the supernatural instinct of the whole people of God. Lumen Gentium 12 says: ‘The whole body of the faithful who have an anointing that comes from the holy one cannot err in matters of belief. This characteristic is shown in the supernatural appreciation of the faith (sensus fidei) on the part of the whole people, when, ‘from the bishops down to the last of the lay faithful,’ they manifest a universal consent in matters of faith and morals.’ A famous example: in the fourth century, when many bishops favored Arianism, the ordinary faithful held to the Nicene faith. Newman wrote about this. It doesn’t mean that doctrine is decided by a poll. Public opinion is not the same as the sensus fidei. The sensus fidei is guided by the Spirit and the Magisterium.”", "Say: “The laity’s work in handing on the faith is great. Parents teach prayer at home. Godparents and sponsors accompany. Catechists, teachers and youth ministers teach. Lay people serve as readers, extraordinary ministers of Holy Communion, in parish councils, as chaplains and at Catholic hospitals. In the world, the laity bring the Gospel to workplaces, politics and culture. Saints such as Thomas More, Frédéric Ozanam, Pier Giorgio Frassati and Gianna Molla were lay people. The Second Vatican Council taught that the laity share in Christ’s priestly, prophetic and kingly roles, and are called to holiness. We are not spectators. Each of us is a link in a chain of faith stretching back to the apostles.”"],
    ask=["Who are the first teachers of faith? (Parents.)", "What is the sensus fidei? (The Spirit-guided instinct for the faith in the whole people of God.)", "Is the sensus fidei a poll? (No; it’s guided by the Spirit and the Magisterium.)"],
    practice=["Make a list of everyone who handed on the faith to you.", "Draw a chain with your name at the end.", "Write three ways you are already handing on the faith, and three you could start.", "Choose one to do this month."],
    close="Pray: “Our Lady of Guadalupe, pray for us.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Thank the person who taught you to pray.”")),
}
